\par\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Asia\par \b \uc2 Yuan Rise Precedes Clinton Visit\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lingling Wei  \par \uc2 870 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 September 2012\par 17:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Top Global Market Stories\par \uc2 DJTGMS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING -- China's yuan has strengthened steadily against the U.S. dollar in recent days ahead of a visit by U.S. Secretary of State Hillary Clinton, offering a reminder of the political sensitivity surrounding the carefully managed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a long-simmering issue between the U.S. and China, with some U.S. businesses and lawmakers accusing Beijing of keeping the yuan artificially weak to help its crucial export sector. Adding to the tensions, the yuan has weakened modestly against the U.S. dollar so far this year after 1 1/2 years of strong appreciation.\par \par \uc2 But recently the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has reversed course, and hit a three-month high on Monday. The yuan is now down 0.7% this year against the dollar, after being down by as much as 1.6% in late July.\par \par \uc2 Traders and analysts attributed the recent rally to a combination of factors, including renewed weakness in the dollar and the potential for heightened Washington pressure on China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies. In particular, they cited Mrs. Clinton's visit and U.S. political sensitivities ahead of a presidential election that is expected to be close and in which both sides have criticized China.\par \par \uc2 "Although the yuan is not viewed as undervalued as it used be, the U.S. will still press China to let the yuan stay strong," said WoonKhien Chia, an analyst at Royal Bank of Scotland Group. The strength in the yuan ahead of Mrs. Clinton's visit is "a constant reminder of the U.S. pressure on China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies," she said.\par \par \uc2 Mrs. Clinton is scheduled to meet with Chinese leaders on Tuesday and Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China keeps the yuan's movements within a tight band in trading within China. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sets the daily rate for the yuan against the dollar, known as the parity rate, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is permitted to move no more than 1% above or below that rate.\par \par \uc2 This year a number of factors have led the PBOC to let the yuan weaken. A cheaper yuan, which makes Chinese goods less expensive in dollar terms, is helping Chinese exporters cope with slowing economic growth and reducing the chances of massive layoffs prior to the country's once-in-a-decade leadership change set to begin later this year. Traders have pressured the yuan over concerns over China's economy and a surge in corporate demand for dollars. Meanwhile, China trade data this year have weakened the argument that the yuan is significantly undervalued.\par \par \uc2 When trading started Monday, the PBOC guided the yuan higher in a move that traders say reflected the broad weakness in the dollar following remarks by U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke last week. Mr. Bernanke's comments further beefed up market expectations for another round of bond purchases by the Fed, known as quantitative easing, which could drive down long-term U.S. interest rates further and weaken the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Dollar Index, which measures the dollar against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , closed Friday at 70.822 from 71.172.\par \par \uc2 Paul Mackel, head of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at HSBC Holdings, said the PBOC recently has appeared to be guiding the yuan's value in accordance with the broad moves in the dollar against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But "at times [the yuan] appears sensitive to the influence of politics," he said. "Going into the presidential election [in the U.S.], there could well be more negative rhetoric."\par \par \uc2 The yuan on Monday finished trading in China 6.3407 to the dollar, after the PBOC set a parity rate of 6.3415 yuan to the dollar. It marked the first time since mid-May the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded stronger against the parity rate.\par \par \uc2 The broader trend this year of a downward shift in the value of the yuan represents a significant reversal in market sentiment. Until late last year it had been viewed by most investors as a surefire bet to rise in value.\par \par \uc2 Still, many market participants don't expect China to let the yuan depreciate in any significant way in the coming months. They say such a move could lead the U.S. to retaliate with trade measures and could exacerbate the amount of capital leaving the country at a time when China is trying to lure in more to bolster its lackluster stock markets.\par \par \uc2 A total of 19 traders among the 30 interviewed by The Wall Street Journal said they expect the dollar-yuan pair to close this year between 6.2750 and 6.3300, generally flat from last year's close. The other 11 fell into a range of 6.3350 to 6.3800, expecting just mild deprecation. The yuan rose 4.7% against the dollar in 2011.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, a recent analysis by Deutsche Bank gave a 76% chance that the yuan will be flat or appreciate slightly in the next 12 months, with the most likely pace of appreciation being 1.5% against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to restrain the scope of depreciation," the analysts said.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Wynne Wang contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJTGMS1120120904e89400059\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
