\par\par \b \uc2 3rd UPDATE: Asian Shares Higher on Stimulus Hopes\b0\par\par\uc2 662 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 August 2012\par 12:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- Asian markets higher on Fed minutes\par \par \uc2 -- Nikkei up 0.5%, Hang Seng Index up 1.2%, S&P ASX 200 up 0.2%\par \par \uc2 -- Hang Seng rallies on strong earnings\par \par \uc2 (Rephrases 1st paragraph, updates prices, company information.)\par \par \uc2 By Daniel Inman\par \par \uc2 Asian markets ended higher on Thursday as hopes for stimulus out of the U.S. outweighed gloomy manufacturing data from China.\par \par \uc2 Minutes from the U.S. Federal Reserve's most recent meeting showed that many members of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy-setting committee were in favor of introducing more stimulus if there is no recovery in economic growth.\par \par \uc2 The minutes were "a reasonably strong statement that they are thinking about QE [Quantitative Easing] and that they are thinking of doing it this year," said \uc2 Joe Bracken\uc2 , head of macro strategies at \uc2 BT Investment Management\uc2  in Australia, which has $36 billion worth of assets under management. "It was surprising on the positive side, because the market had discounted the Fed launching anything."\par \par \uc2 Markets stumbled during the session when \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's preliminary China purchasing managers index for August came out--showing a fall to 47.8, compared with a final reading of 49.3 in July. A score below 50 suggests a contraction in manufacturing activity and the latest numbers were at a nine-month low.\par \par \uc2 Most markets initially weakened on the data, though they soon picked up as hopes for quantitative easing once again took hold.\par \par \uc2 "Investors knew that China's economy was slowing and shrugged off the PMI data after a while. They started to pin their hopes on possible government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  and abroad," said Zheng Ping, an analyst at Minsheng Securities in China.\par \par \uc2 One market that didn't recover well after the data was Australia, which is particularly sensitive to Chinese economic data. The S&P ASX 200 gained just 0.2% to 4383.70.\par \par \uc2 Local earnings reports moved some stocks: \uc2 Fortescue Metals Group\uc2  gained 2.2% after its earnings met expectations, while Qantas Airways added 2.6% as the market was impressed by the company's underlying profit.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar however, did strengthen against its U.S. counterpart, on the easing hopes--to $1.0518, compared with $1.0439 late on Wednesday. The dollar also lost ground against the South Korean won, at 1,130 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The most significant move though was between the dollar and the yen, as the greenback weakened 0.9% overnight against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , stabilizing at Y78.62. When the Fed attempts to push down U.S. bond yields, it reduces the incentive to hold dollars over yen.\par \par \uc2 The stronger yen initially weighed on the Nikkei, but the market turned positive to finish the day up 0.5% at 9178.12. South Korea's Kospi was up 0.4% at 1942.54.\par \par \uc2 Along with easing hopes, corporate earnings took center stage in Hong Kong, where the Hang Seng Index rose 1.2% to 20,132.24. Local developers soared after releasing strong first-half earnings reports, with \uc2 Henderson Land Development\uc2  jumping 4.7% and Wharf (Holdings) also advancing 4.7%. In mainland China, the Shanghai Composite ended 0.3% higher at 2113.07.\par \par \uc2 In Vietnam, the Ho Chi Minh Index lost 4.2%, as investor confidence plunged with another arrest of a chief executive, as police in Ho Chi Minh City detained Ly Xuan Hai of Asia Commercial Bank. Banks were hit particularly hard: Military Bank lost 4.3%, \uc2 Sacombank\uc2  was 4.8% lower, and Eximbank finished 4.8% lower.\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 23-08-12 1045GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1106 4125 5037 62560   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120823e88n000o5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
