\par\par \b \uc2 REFILE-Yuan rises on stronger euro, traders still fear future declines\b0\par\par\uc2 395 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juli 2012\par 08:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Removes unecessary word from headline)\par \par \uc2 * Euro gains on \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief comments\par \par \uc2 * Market players remain cautious over further falls in yuan\par \par \uc2 * 6.40-level will be a strong support\par \par \uc2 By Chen Yixin and Pete Sweeney\par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, July 27 (Reuters) - China's yuan rose slightly on Friday after the euro gained in global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, but market participants said the yuan still faces strong depreciation pressure in coming days.\par \par \uc2 The euro steadied in early Asian trading on Friday after rallying on \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Mario Draghi's vow to hold the euro zone together.\par \par \uc2 Due to the gain in euro, the U.S. dollar index fell slightly to 82.793 against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after slumping 0.9 percent overnight.\par \par \uc2 Spot yuan was at 6.3784 per dollar at midday, 57 pips firmer than Thursday's close. \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  set the daily midpoint at 6.3325, 56 pips stronger than Thursday's fix.\par \par \uc2 "The recent yuan rebounds over the past several days are not very strong, which indicates the market is still concerned that the yuan will fall further," said a dealer at a Chinese commercial bank in Shanghai.\par \par \uc2 Market players are also concerned that dollar demand from large oil companies could also drag down the yuan.\par \par \uc2 But dealers also said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not allow a sharp fall in the yuan, adding that the 6.40 against the dollar will be a strong support level in the near term. If the spot yuan breaks through the 6.40 level, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be forced to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they said.\par \par \uc2 However, Liu Dongliang, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  Merchants Bank\uc2  in Shenzhen, said in a report that China's yuan could potentially depreciate to as weak as 6.45 or 6.50 per dollar in the second half of this year, given the economic environment both at home and abroad.\par \par \uc2 The one-year non-deliverable forward fell slightly to 6.4325 by midday on Friday from Thursday's 6.4365.\par \par \uc2 Offshore yuan traded in Hong Kong, CNH, remained close to the onshore price at 6.3820.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Eric Meijer)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-CHINA-YUAN-MIDDAY/  (CORRECTED) | ABN | C | GRO | MTL | SOF | D | E | RBN | M | O | OIL | ABX | BNX | FUN | Z | RNP | DNP | PTD | PEN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120730e87r0008b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
