\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ(7/26) China Shifts Yuan's Course, Risking A Clash With US\b0\par\par\uc2 798 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juli 2012\par 02:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par A7\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL) \par    By Bob Davis and Lingling Wei \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BEIJING -- \f28 \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is starting to guide the yuan downward against the dollar after two years of trying to boost its value, reflecting concern in Beijing over China's slowing economy -- and risking a political fight with the U.S.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  guided the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to its weakest level of the year on Wednesday against the U.S. dollar, the third straight day of a push to bring down the yuan's value. Overall, the yuan has fallen 1.1% against the dollar this year after rising 4.7% against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year.\par \par \uc2 The PBOC didn't respond to requests for comment, and it isn't clear whether the trend will continue in coming days and weeks. A similar steering of the yuan to weaken was seen in early May, which was then followed by a return to the usual practice of seeking to let it remain stable or to strengthen against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Traders and analysts say the shift in the last few days is aimed at helping exporters cope with slowing sales and reducing the chances of major layoffs ahead of a major -- and sensitive -- once-a-decade Chinese leadership change set to begin later this year. A cheaper yuan makes Chinese goods less expensive in dollar terms.\par \par \uc2 Premier Wen Jiabao last week warned that "the task of promoting full employment will be very heavy and we must make greater efforts to achieve it."\par \par \uc2 The psychological impact of a weakened yuan "will be strong," said Dariusz Kowalczyk, a \uc2 Credit Agricole\uc2  senior economist based in Hong Kong. "It creates an impression that Beijing is so focused on minimizing risks to growth that it is ignoring any U.S. pressure."\par \par \uc2 But a fall in the yuan is bound to keep it a political issue in the U.S. during an election year. Treasury Undersecretary Lael Brainard said last week that China should "avoid persistent exchange-rate misalignment," although she credited China with making some progress on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  front. Republican presidential challenger Mitt Romney has said he would name China a "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  violator," a designation that could trigger a confrontation with China.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, on Wednesday the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  reiterated its contention that China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is "moderately undervalued" against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The IMF didn't specify the degree of undervaluation in its annual assessment of China's economy, but individuals who track the IMF say the yuan is likely to be judged to be undervalued by less than 10%.\par \par \uc2 China's representative to the IMF, Zhang Tao, said the IMF's characterization "is not consistent with the reality." He said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is "roughly in equilibrium," meaning it didn't have to rise much more, if at all.\par \par \uc2 Beijing has its eyes trained on not just the dollar. The European Union is China's No. 2 export market after the U.S., and the yuan has appreciated 5.6% against the euro since January. During that time, the dollar has risen 6.9% against the euro. By reducing the value of the yuan against the dollar, the PBOC slows its rise against the euro.\par \par \uc2 That provides some help to Chinese exporters who are feeling the effects of Europe's continuing debt crisis. "We've seen a sharp drop in demand from Europe," said Xu Peng, an official at Huihong International Group, a state-owned textiles company, which has started to take out trade insurance to guard against potential nonpayment from European clients.\par \par \uc2 The PBOC sets a daily rate, called the parity rate, for the yuan's trade against the dollar in the mainland \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  then lets the yuan rise or fall by 1% in daily trading.\par \par \uc2 Monday, the PBOC set the parity rate weaker against the dollar than Friday, according to data provider ChinaScope Financial in Hong Kong. It continued that pattern the next two days.\par \par \uc2 Traders since late last year have frequently bid down the yuan's value, and in recent days have brought it close to the bottom of the 1% limit. It closed at 6.3885 to the dollar in Asian trading Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 With $3.24 trillion in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, the Chinese government has the wherewithal to assure the continuing steady rise of the yuan. But such a move could be interpreted as a signal money is fleeing China.\par \par \uc2 "Authorities certainly don't want to signal any weakness," said Brookings Institution \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  scholar Eswar Prasad. "So they will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  very cautiously."\par \par \uc2 Subscribe to WSJ: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires }}}\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 25, 2012 20:21 ET (00:21 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020120726e87q00017\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
