\par\par \b \uc2 China Yuan Down Late on Lingering Worries Over Economic Growth\b0\par\par\uc2 515 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juli 2012\par 11:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                   Vs Parity  Previous \par USD/CNY Central Parity   6.3209               6.3195 \par USD/CNY OTC 0830 GMT     6.3686    +0.75%     6.3659 \par             High         6.3691    +0.76% \par             Low          6.3648    +0.69% \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SHANGHAI--China's yuan was lower against the dollar late Wednesday, as the U.S. unit advanced against the euro overnight and because of lingering concerns about China's economic growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 On the over-the-counter market, the dollar was at CNY6.3686 at 0830 GMT, up from 6.3659 late Tuesday. It traded in a narrow range of CNY6.3648 to CNY6.3691.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  set the dollar/yuan central parity rate at 6.3209, up slightly from Tuesday's 6.3195. The U.S. unit gained sharply against the euro Tuesday in New York on concerns that Europe isn't doing enough to solve Spain's debt problems.\par \par \uc2 "Despite a slightly higher fixing today, which doesn't reflect the greenback's strong gain last night, investors are still buying and holding the dollar given worries over a slowing Chinese economy and uncertainty about whether it has bottomed out," said a Shanghai-based trader at a local bank.\par \par \uc2 China's economy grew 8.1% in the first quarter, the slowest quarterly expansion in three years. Recent economic data, including consumer inflation and imports, have showed domestic demand continued to worsen in the second quarter.\par \par \uc2 Economists widely expect China's economy in the second quarter to have expanded at its slowest pace since the first quarter of 2009. China is scheduled to release gross domestic product data on Friday.\par \par \uc2 However, banks have treaded carefully in recent sessions as the yuan has posted a more than 1% depreciation against the dollar so far this year.\par \par \uc2 "Banks don't dare to long the dollar too much, which will make the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  uneasy," said a Shanghai-based trader at a foreign bank.\par \par \uc2 "The recent central parity rates suggest that the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  wants to keep the yuan stable at present when sentiment toward China is poor," he said.\par \par \uc2 The recent trading patterns show that the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has set the psychological resistance for the dollar at CNY6.3800, traders say.\par \par \uc2 "Once the dollar approaches the level, some state-owned banks will put up a huge block of dollars for sale," said the foreign-bank trader.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tends to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market through big state-owned banks, traders say.\par \par \uc2 Looking ahead, traders said they expect the dollar to consolidate between CNY6.3600 and CNY6.3700 in the short term.\par \par \uc2 Offshore, one-year dollar/yuan nondeliverable forward contracts fell to 6.4145/6.4175 from 6.4170/6.4220 late Tuesday, implying a 0.7% fall by the yuan over the next year.\par \par \uc2 In the offshore yuan market in Hong Kong, where the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  floats freely, the dollar was at CNY6.3635, barely changed from CNY6.3630 late Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Write to Rose Yu at rose.yu@dowjones.com [ 11-07-12 0907GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1153   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120711e87b000j3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
