\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ: China Facing 'Strategic Opportunity' to Open Capital Account--PBOC Official\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lingling Wei   \par \uc2 354 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juni 2012\par 06:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI--China currently faces a "strategic opportunity" to remove restrictions on investment flows, a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said Saturday, while stressing that opening up the capital account will be a gradual process.\par \par \uc2 Loosening controls on capital flows could help Chinese companies expand overseas and promote global use of the Chinese yuan, Sheng Songcheng, head of the statistics department at the \uc2 People's Bank\uc2  of China, told reporters on the sidelines of the Lujiazui Forum, a gathering of financial executives and regulators.\par \par \uc2 (This story and related background material will be available on The Wall Street Journal website, WSJ.com.)\par \par \uc2 Mr. Sheng stressed a "gradual" approach to liberalizing cross-border fund flows. For instance, the government could take one to three years to relax restrictions on outbound direct investments and three to five years to loosen controls on commercial credits, Mr. Sheng suggested. To develop its financial markets, China could open up its real-estate sector and stock and bond markets in five to 10 years, he said.\par \par \uc2 Capital-account liberalization has been a topic of intense debate within China in recent months. Some analysts have said Beijing shouldn't make the move until after it completes reforming its financial sector, including letting market forces decide how to allocate capital and resources.\par \par \uc2 "There may never be a proper timing for pushing the reform, if the country waits until conditions mature for the interest rate and exchange rate to be decided by the market," Mr. Sheng said.\par \par \uc2 China has been slowly relaxing its controls on capital flows by allowing the market to play a bigger role in deciding the yuan's exchange rate and pushing for wider use of the yuan in cross-border trade settlement and investment.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Sheng said the PBOC has reduced its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, pointing to the slower growth in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. "It's not frequent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as some people have suggested," he said.\par \par \uc2 Write to Lingling Wei at lingling.wei@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020120630e86u000gp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
