\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories of the Day\b0\par\par\uc2 622 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juni 2012\par 04:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par 2ND UPDATE: GROWTH PACT HELD UP AS ITALY, SPAIN CALL FOR IMMEDIATE HELP \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Italy, Spain were driving a hard bargain at a European Union summit early Friday refusing to sign off on a 120 billion euro growth pact until urgent action was agreed to help ease their soaring borrowing costs. (published 0139 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: JAPAN OUTPUT, PRICE DATA SUGGEST RECOVERY YET TO TAKE HOLD \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese industrial production fell a worse-than-expected 3.1% in May from the previous month while consumer prices were lower in the period, the government said Friday, in a sign that a recovery in the domestic economy has yet to take hold. (published 0041 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par DATA SNAP: SOUTH KOREA INDUSTRIAL OUTPUT UP 1.1% ON MONTH; BEATS FORECAST \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's industrial production increaed at a faster pace in May than in the preceding month, beating forecasts, government data showed Thursday. (published 2303 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIAN PRIVATE-SECTOR CREDIT +0.5% IN MAY VS APRIL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Credit to the Australian private sector rose a seasonally adjusted 0.5% in May from April and rose 4.0% from a year earlier, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Friday. (published 0133 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  CHIEF: WILL CONTINUE PRUDENT MONETARY POLICY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China will continue with its "prudent" monetary policy and fine-tune it to cope with economic conditions, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central-bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  chief said Friday. (published 0109 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INTERVIEW: CHINA 2Q ECONOMIC GROWTH MAY SLOW TO 7.8%-7.9% - CITIC BANK STRATEGIST \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's economic growth might have slowed to 7.8%-7.9% in the second quarter as worsening global demand continues to hurt domestic companies' manufacturing, Pang Aihua, a strategist with \f28 \uc2 \f2 China Citic Bank Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., told Dow Jones Newswires. (published 0106 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA SAYS EU SHOULD REMOVE TRADE, INVESTMENT BARRIERS - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China has urged Brussels to "remove barriers" and allow Chinese companies in to help stimulate jobs and growth in Europe, China Daily reported Friday, citing an official at the Ministry of Commerce. (published 0112 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par HOLLANDE:FRANCE WANTS DEEPER EUROPEAN INTEGRATION ONLY WITHIN EURO ZONE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 France is ready to allow for power transfer to European Union institutions only if the process is limited to the 17 euro-zone countries, French President Francois Hollande said Thursday, as he called for a quick implementation of a budgetary and banking union within the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  zone. (published 0042 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par U.K., FRANCE, GERMANY FAIL TO AGREE ON EU PATENT COURT HQ \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.K., France and Germany failed to agree on the location of a new European Union patent court during a summit Thursday, providing a potential hurdle to agreement on an EU growth pact, officials said. (published 0032 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par DATA SNAP: UK CONSUMERS STUCK IN DEEPEST FUNK FOR 40 YEARS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Britons have not been so gloomy about their prospects since records began 40 years ago, according to a survey released Friday. (published 2301 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ UPDATE: BANKING GROUP ASKS UK TO EXAMINE LIBOR PROCESS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The process for setting one of the world's most important interest rates veered toward a shake-up when the U.K. banking group responsible for the rate asked government officials to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (published 2332 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ UPDATE: U.S. CLEARS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CHINA\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FROM IRAN OIL SANCTIONS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. exempted China from penalties for doing business with Tehran as the latest set of U.S. sanctions targeting Iran's oil exports took effect on Thursday. (published 2338 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par DBS GROUP TRIPLES U.S. COMMERCIAL PAPER PROGRAM TO $15 BILLION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DBS Group Holdings Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Friday said the banking group has raised the size of its existing U.S. commercial paper program to $15 billion from $5 billion at present. (published 2327 GMT) [ 29-06-12 0200GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120629e86t0003n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
