\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Short-covering supports;  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2  data adds to gloom\b0\par\par\uc2 1,178 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juni 2012\par 07:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Indonesia c.bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  supports rupiah, IDR NDFs hit\par \par \uc2 * Won up on stop-loss dollar sales, exporters\par \par \uc2 * Sing dlr edges up, but F1 IPO delay weighs\par \par \uc2 (Adds details, updates prices)\par \par \uc2 By Jongwoo Cheon\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, June 1 (Reuters) - Emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose on Friday on short-covering, and the battered units were headed for another week of losses as weak data from China compounded fears about global growth.\par \par \uc2 Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was spotted selling dollars to support the rupiah, pushing down the one-month dollar/rupiah non-deliverable forwards (NDFs) as offshore investors unwound hedges. The rupiah also found some support from lower-than-expected inflation data, dealers said.\par \par \uc2 The South Korean won gained on stop-loss dollar selling and exporters' demand for settlements, while market players stayed cautious over the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities' \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The local unit also found support from dollar supplies linked to foreign investors' recent stock purchases.\par \par \uc2 But the outlook for emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  stayed weak as the euro zone's persistent debt crisis cast a shadow over markets.\par \par \uc2 China's official purchasing managers' index fell more than expected to 50.4 in May, the weakest reading this year and down from April's 13-month high, in the latest sign that output in the world's second-biggest economy is cooling.\par \par \uc2 "It seems that the slowdown of the Chinese economy is increasingly pronounced," said Dariusz Kowalczyk, senior economist and strategist at \uc2 Credit Agricole\uc2  CIB in Hong Kong, adding that second-quarter growth would likely slow to 7.5 percent from a year ago or even lower.\par \par \uc2 "We expect local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fall as a result across the Asian space, as the region is likely to suffer more from a China slowdown than from euro zone recession in terms of export loss," he said.\par \par \uc2 Reflecting this view, South Korean exports fell again in May, hurt by a drop-off in demand from China, the United States and Europe.\par \par \uc2 Indonesian exports fell for the first time in nearly three years in April, pushing the trade balance into negative on falling value of oil and rubber shipments amid waning demand from Europe and U.S.\par \par \uc2 U.S. data stoked concerns a labour market recovery was losing steam as private payroll growth picked up only slightly and claims for jobless benefits rose last week.\par \par \uc2 The Taiwan dollar, the Indian rupee, the Singapore dollar and the Malaysian ringgit have lost about 0.8 percent against the U.S. dollar so far this week, according to Thomson Reuters data.\par \par \uc2 The rupee is set for a ninth weekly loss and the Singapore dollar is heading for a fifth losing week, data showed.\par \par \uc2 Last month, most emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  suffered from their largest monthly losses since September last year on mounting worries that Spain may need external aid to shore up its struggling banking sector.\par \par \uc2 Greece also stays as concerns ahead of a June 17 election. Opinion polls contradicted each other on Friday, one day before a ban on their publication kicks in, sowing confusion as to which party is the front-runner to win the election that may decide whether the country will stay in the euro zone.\par \par \uc2 RUPIAH\par \par \uc2 Dollar/rupiah spot indicative price fell on continuous \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while one-month dollar/rupiah NDFs slumped 2.2 percent to 9,460.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday sold dollar/rupiah from 9,400 to 9,300, dealers said. Officials from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  were not immediately available for comment.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , along with the lower inflation number, helped stabilise the rupiah, dealers said. Indonesian inflation in May surprisingly slowed to 4.45 percent from a year ago, lower than a forecast of 4.57 percent.\par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia Governor Darmin Nasution said inflation will trend to decline going forward.\par \par \uc2 "The market is better than yesterday. People placed bids for (dollar/rupiah) at 9,350 for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said a Jakarta-based dealer, waiting for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell at that level.\par \par \uc2 In actual transactions, the pair was bid above 9,600 earlier in the week, dealers said.\par \par \uc2 PHILIPPINE PESO\par \par \uc2 Dollar/peso initially rose on worries about Spain, but the pair turned lower as interbank players took profits.\par \par \uc2 Those players saw the recent gains in dollar/peso as excessive and sold the pair above 43.70, dealers said.\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE DOLLAR\par \par \uc2 U.S. dollar/Singapore dollar slid on profit-taking and caution over U.S. dollar-selling by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore (MAS).\par \par \uc2 But its downside was limited by a delay in Formula One's initial public offering (IPO) and the slowing China's economy. The motor sport racing company delayed its Singapore IPO worth up to $3 billion due to a weak market, denting inflow hopes.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, \uc2 Barclays\uc2  recommended buying Singapore dollar/Taiwan dollar as relative growth and inflation trends suggest the MAS has somewhat greater near-term room and incentive than the Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to allow \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  outperformance.\par \par \uc2 Barclays\uc2  advised purchasing the cross pair spot at 23.19 with an initial target of 24.00 in one month and a stop-loss at 22.60, the low of early 2011.\par \par \uc2 Singapore dollar/Taiwan dollar rose 0.3 percent to 23.2197.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par \par \uc2 Change on the day at 0655 GMT \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Japan yen        78.45          78.30       -0.19\par  Sing dlr        1.2870         1.2884       +0.11\par  Taiwan dlr      29.900         29.860       -0.13\par  Korean won     1177.00        1180.30       +0.28\par  Baht             31.82          31.82       +0.00\par  Peso             43.41          43.50       +0.22\par  Rupiah         9350.00        9390.00       +0.43\par  Rupee            55.84          56.08       +0.43\par  Ringgit         3.1825         3.1820       -0.02\par  Yuan            6.3705         6.3690       -0.02\par  Change so far in 2012\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        78.45          76.92       -1.95\par  Sing dlr        1.2870         1.2969       +0.77\par  Taiwan dlr      29.900         30.290       +1.30\par  Korean won     1177.00        1151.80       -2.14\par  Baht             31.82          31.55       -0.85\par  Peso             43.41          43.84       +1.00\par  Rupiah         9350.00        9060.00       -3.10\par  Rupee            55.84          53.08       -4.94\par  Ringgit         3.1825         3.1685       -0.44\par  Yuan            6.3705       