\par\par \b \uc2 DJ PBOC Vice Gov: Time To Let Market 'More Or Less' Set Yuan Rate\b0\par\par\uc2 757 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 April 2012\par 09:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 --PBOC Vice Gov. Yi Gang says \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  should reduce \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, giving market greater role\par \par \uc2 --Also warns, however, against exchange-rate "overshooting," as seen historically by Japan\par \par \uc2 --In debate with IMF official, Yi argues that China has made progress rebalancing and domestic consumption is underestimated\par \par \uc2 (Adds analysis of PBOC's position within the Chinese government in seventh to eighth paragraphs.)\par \par \uc2 By Aaron Back\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--China should now let the market play a greater role in determining the yuan's exchange rate and reduce its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  Vice Governor Yi Gang said Saturday.\par \par \uc2 "I think that it is time to let the market, more or less, decide the rate," he said on a panel during the spring meetings of the International Monetary Fund and the World Bank.\par \par \uc2 In a debate with a senior IMF official, Yi also argued that China has made substantial progress in rebalancing its economy and that domestic consumption is understated by official statistics.\par \par \uc2 In discussing the exchange rate, Yi noted that over the last two quarters there has persistently been "two-way" pressures on the yuan, with markets for certain periods of time expecting yuan depreciation.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, the PBOC widened the yuan's daily trading band to 1% from 0.5% previously, sending a signal that it is permitted to allow greater flexibility for the yuan exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, the PBOC will continue to exert considerable influence on the yuan rate by setting a daily reference rate against the dollar, around which the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is allowed to trade within the band.\par \par \uc2 The PBOC has long been an advocate of greater yuan flexibility, but it has been opposed by other government departments such as the Ministry of Commerce, which represents the nation's exporters.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not independent and major decisions on the exchange rate and other policy matters require the approval of the country's top political leadership within the Communist Party.\par \par \uc2 During the same panel, Yi also gave an indication of why China would want to maintain some control over the exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 Discussing lessons that China has learned from Japan's stagnation, Yi said China wants to avoid an "overshooting" of its exchange rate. Chinese officials have frequently argued that foreign pressure on Japan in the 1980s led to an overvalued yen, contributing to the country's economic downturn later on.\par \par \uc2 Yi also said that China wants to avoid Japan-style asset bubbles, as a burst can lead to an intractable "balance-sheet recession."\par \par \uc2 On the panel, Yi debated with IMF Deputy Managing Director Naoyuki Shinohara on the reasons for China's declining capital account surplus, which fell to less than 3% of GDP in 2011 from over 10% in 2007.\par \par \uc2 Yi argued that around two-thirds of the decline is due to long-term structural factors, while Shinohara maintained that it was unclear how much was due to such fundamentals and how much due to short-term cyclical factors.\par \par \uc2 The exchange between the two men reflected a long-running debate between China and the IMF.\par \par \uc2 The IMF has argued for years that the declining surplus is mostly due to "cyclical" factors, mainly lower demand for Chinese goods in the U.S. and Europe. China's surplus is due to rebound once growth in those regions recovers, the IMF has consistently said.\par \par \uc2 However, the IMF has recently moderated its view on the subject, cutting its long-term estimates for the surplus and reviewing its stance that the yuan is substantially undervalued.\par \par \uc2 Yi cited rising labor and environmental costs, increasingly expensive urban housing, greater spending on social services and the appreciation of the yuan over the last seven years as factors contributing to the rebalancing.\par \par \uc2 Shinohara made the point that China remains unbalanced domestically, with extraordinarily high levels of investment and suppressed consumption.\par \par \uc2 Yi replied that official Chinese statistics may understate the level of consumption in China. Any visitor to a Chinese city will see that "consumption is booming," he said.\par \par \uc2 Shinohara said that consumption may be growing quickly, but so is GDP, and the ratio of consumption to GDP is in fact falling.\par \par \uc2 "There may be a problem with the data, I don't know, but that is what the statistics show," Shinohara said.\par \par \uc2 -By Aaron Back, Dow Jones Newswires; (8610) 8400-7701; aaron.back@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 22-04-12 0721GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020120422e84m00008\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
