\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  HORIZONS: QE3 And The Looming Danger Of  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  War 2\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michael J. Casey   \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN   \par \uc2 972 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 April 2012\par 16:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Like Pavlov's dog, which would salivate at the sound of a bell, U.S. stock market investors will these days launch into a buying frenzy at the faintest hint of more monetary stimulus.\par \par \uc2 But if \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  officials' assurances of continued support for the U.S. recovery have released feel-good endorphins among wishful thinkers on Wall Street, their subtle admissions that a third round of "quantitative easing," or QE3, is not off the table may equally have sent emerging market central bankers into a cold sweat.\par \par \uc2 Financial authorities in developing countries fear their economies will be in the line of fire if U.S. economic conditions deteriorate so much that the Fed again starts buying bonds to anchor U.S. interest rates. They know from experience that many of the dollars the Fed injects into the financial system would flow offshore and into their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , whose higher yields are more attractive to investors. This "hot money" would push up their exchange rates to the detriment of their exporters and leave their capital markets vulnerable to volatile turns in sentiment.\par \par \uc2 If these policymakers were to respond to QE3 as many did to QE2--by buying dollars in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, cutting interest rates or otherwise quelling exchange rate appreciation--they would revive what Brazilian Finance Minister Guido Mantega alarmingly described in 2010 as a "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war." With their export leaders complaining of a loss of competitiveness, central bankers would come under immense political pressure to protect vital industries and to go soft on inflation, often a byproduct of a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And this time, global conditions are such that their actions could set off a self-perpetuating cycle of competing devaluations that does considerable damage to the world trading system.\par \par \uc2 Unlike in 2010, China's economy is now slowing. Meanwhile, Europe is in an austerity-led recession and the modestly improved U.S. economy faces a major test when steep fiscal cuts take effect at year-end. QE3 could easily land in a world of weakening global demand, which means exporters from places like South Korea, Turkey, Brazil and South Africa would be fighting over a shrinking pie. The temptation to fight lost competitiveness with market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rather than by curtailing domestic costs would be great.\par \par \uc2 Making matters worse, investors now have fewer alternatives as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bets. They won't want to chase the euro higher, not amid the ever-present threat of a debt meltdown in Spain. There is no upside in the Swiss franc, now anchored by a minimum CHF1.20 price that the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  is vowing to pay for euros. And ever since the 2010 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war, many governments--most notably Brazil's--are taxing foreign capital and using other measures to curtail hot money inflows.\par \par \uc2 Money will seek the path of the least resistance. So \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of emerging market and commodity producers with a less \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange regime--Australia's or Mexico's, for example--could bear the brunt. The result would be a matrix of grossly distorted exchange rates and heightened international tension, all of which could ultimately encourage trade sanctions.\par \par \uc2 China's actions will be key. In the past, the People' Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the yuan from rising too rapidly exacerbated the Fed-induced \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war. China's closed capital account and tight management of exchange rates kept the yuan off limits for speculators, steering them into smaller nations' \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Now, after widening the band within which the yuan can freely trade, Chinese authorities have hinted at more flexibility. Yet the international praise they earned for this \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  "liberalization" could prove premature. Beijing can still fix the midpoint of the newly widened trading range and may well choose to hold it in place. China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains too tightly controlled to be the safety valve the world needs.\par \par \uc2 Jim Rickards, author of the recently published "\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wars," believes we are in a phase not unlike two previous cycles of beggar-they-neighbor competitive devaluations: that which spanned the 1920s and 30s, and the 1970s. It is up to the Fed, he says, to avoid a disastrous rerun by reaffirming the strength of the dollar and disavowing more quantitative easing.\par \par \uc2 We can expect a similar buck-stops-with-the-Fed narrative when finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors from the BRICS countries of Brazil, Russia, India, China and South Africa meet on the sidelines of this week's meetings of the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  in Washington. But Chairman Ben Bernanke and his colleague at the Fed have tended to dismiss the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war rhetoric and argue instead that the problem lies with emerging market central banks paying too little attention to inflation and too much to exchange rates.\par \par \uc2 All this will be moot, of course, if the U.S. economy continues to add jobs and the Fed takes no further action. For the sake of global stability, the whole world should pray that's the case.\par \par \uc2 (Michael Casey is managing editor for the Americas at DJ \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trader, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  news service jointly produced by Dow Jones Newswires and The Wall Street Journal. His new book on the global financial system, "The Unfair Trade," will be published on May 29. He can be reached at michael.j.casey@dowjones.com.)\par \par \uc2 (TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAmericas@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.) [ 04-18-12 1030ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020120418e84i0009l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
