\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ UPDATE: PBOC Chief Signals Yuan May Continue To Appreciate\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bob Davis and Lingling Wei  \par \uc2 Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL  \par \uc2 1,169 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2012\par 14:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING (Dow Jones)--China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Zhou Xiaochuan said market forces will play a bigger role in setting the value of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  but that it hadn't yet reached an equilibrium level, suggesting that the yuan may still have room to appreciate.\par \par \uc2 "Is renminbi appreciation over?" Mr. Zhou asked rhetorically at an annual news briefing in Beijing, using the formal name for the yuan. Although he didn't directly answer his question, his explanation indicated that he thought the answer was probably no.\par \par \uc2 "I think [the value of the yuan] mainly depends on supply and demand," Mr. Zhou said. But getting to that point "is not that simple."\par \par \uc2 (This story and related background material will be available on The Wall Street Journal website, WSJ.com.)\par \par \uc2 "The closer the RMB moves to an equilibrium level, the bigger role the market plays," he added. At that point, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could reduce \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "in an orderly manner." The equilibrium is the point at which supply and demand for a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  match, suggesting that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is fairly valued.\par \par \uc2 Since June 2010, when the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  allowed the yuan to float somewhat, the PBOC has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to guide the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher. But the future direction of the yuan has become increasingly in doubt as China's trade surplus has shrunk over recent months.\par \par \uc2 In February, China ran a $31.5 billion trade deficit, the largest at least since 2000. While the weeklong Lunar New Year holiday in January disrupted China's usual export-and-import flow for the first two months of the year, the trade deficit for January and February totaled $4.25 billion, compared with a deficit of $890 million in the first two months of last year.\par \par \uc2 The trade figures and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  valuation are linked economically and politically. Economists often judge whether a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is undervalued by gauging the size of the trade surplus and how much a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have to gain in value to eliminate or sharply scale back that surplus. Politically, exporters and their allies fight harder against appreciation, which makes exports more expensive as the trade surplus narrows.\par \par \uc2 The yuan has depreciated roughly 0.5% against the dollar since the beginning of 2012, after gaining 4.7% last year.\par \par \uc2 While bank analysts have argued that China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was likely to increase in value this year, though at a slower pace than in 2011, Monday's news conference was the first time China's central bankers have weighed in on the subject publicly. The briefing was well-attended, with reporters jostling each other and grabbing the microphone to try to get their questions answered.\par \par \uc2 The PBOC probably isn't the last word on the subject. Significant economic policies are decided at the State Council, the government's highest administrative body, with considerable input by the standing committee of China's Politburo, the Communist Party's top group. It isn't known whether either body has recently debated \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate policy.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has successfully advocated for revaluation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a way to help China remake its economy so it relies less on exports and investment in capital-intensive industries and more on domestic demand. A strengthening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  helps that transition because it makes exports more expensive, thus helping to wean China off its dependence on low-margin exported goods, and makes imports less expensive. The latter effectively puts more money into the pockets of Chinese residents and makes consumer goods cheaper to buy domestically.\par \par \uc2 By referring to the continuing need to "rebalance" the economy, Mr. Zhou was essentially making the case for further appreciation. "It won't be a short period of time before the imbalances in the global economy, including China's, get corrected," said Mr. Zhou. "During that process, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a participant in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market."\par \par \uc2 To deal with a slowing Chinese economy, Mr. Zhou said China has "lots of room" to cut the reserves that Chinese banks are required to hold, which would free up money to lend. The PBOC has cut the reserve requirement ratio twice since the end of last year.\par \par \uc2 UBS\uc2  economist Wang Tao said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's words also needed to be interpreted in a political context. "Given the current political and economic environment, with Europe going into a recession and the U.S. in an election season where there is China bashing, it's very hard for China to let renminbi depreciate," she said.\par \par \uc2 How rapidly the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will appreciate this year is hard to tell. Ms. Wang and a number of other analysts figure 2% to 3%. Mr. Zhou didn't give a hint, though the recent trade figures suggest that the pace of appreciation is likely to decline from last year.\par \par \uc2 PBOC Deputy Governor Yi Gang said the trade deficit was "a very positive sign" that the yuan's exchange rate is moving close to an equilibrium and that traders no longer expected that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would move only upward on any given day. He said the PBOC needed to improve \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedures "to increase the two-way flexibility of the renminbi's exchange rate."\par \par \uc2 One way to do that would be to increase the daily trading range, which now limits the movement of the yuan to 0.5% from the price fixed by the PBOC. While such a move has long been expected, neither Mr. Yi nor Mr. Zhou said it was imminent.\par \par \uc2 On other issues, China said it would continue to diversify the holdings in its $3.2 trillion in foreign reserves by buying euro-denominated securities.\par \par \uc2 "We have preserved and increased the value of our investments in the euro zone and Europe," said Mr. Yi, who also heads the State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which manages China's reserve horde.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury data from earlier in March suggested that the percentage of dollar holdings in China's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves has fallen to a decade low of 54% in the year ended June 30, 2011, from 65% in 2010.\par \par \uc2 Moving away from the dollar is tough, though, given the huge size of China's holdings. Heavy purchases of yen-denominated securities could boost the value of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , despite Tokyo's determination to drive down the yen's value. "If there is a period when the Japanese side worries about whether us investing too much would cause the yen to rise in value, we would invest less," said Mr. Yi.\par \par \uc2 On a lighter note, Mr. Zhou waved off speculation that he might be a candidate for World Bank president, noting that the position has always gone to an American, and he had no reason to believe that would change. "So I don't think you need to worry about this."\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 12, 2012 09:43 ET (13:43 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020120312e83c006le\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
