\par\par \b \uc2 2nd UPDATE: CHINA NPC: \uc2 PBOC\uc2  Zhou: Room For Further RRR Cuts In Theory\b0\par\par\uc2 713 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2012\par 06:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- PBOC Gov. Zhou Xiaochuan says there is "lots of room" to cut banks' reserve ratio further, in theory.\par \par \uc2 -- He says such a move depends on factors such as the international balance of payments.\par \par \uc2 -- \uc2 PBOC\uc2  Vice Gov. Yi Gang says China will increase investment in Europe.\par \par \uc2 -- Yi says yuan's value is up to the market to decide.\par \par \uc2 (Adds comments on Japanese government bonds in the 10th paragraph, on the yuan exchange rate in the 13th paragraph, and on China's trade balance in the 14th paragraph.)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Aaron Back and Shen Hong \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BEIJING (Dow Jones)--China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor said Monday that in theory, there is "lots of room" to cut banks' reserve requirement ratio further, but whether the People's Bank of China makes such cuts will depend on factors such as the international balance of payments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At a wide-ranging news conference during the National People's Congress, the annual meetings of China's legislature, top \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  officials reiterated Beijing's pledge to invest more in crisis-ridden Europe, saying investments in the region have so far maintained their value or even generated returns. They also said that the authorities expect two-way movements in the yuan's exchange rate, and that the value of the Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would increasingly be determined by market forces.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 PBOC Gov. Zhou Xiaochuan said adjustments to the share of deposits banks must keep with the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  aren't necessarily indicative of monetary easing or tightening, but are instead more related to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's management of liquidity, such as its purchases of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Zhou added that cuts to banks' reserve ratio aren't meant to boost confidence in the stock market or the property sector.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He also said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  must take international capital flows into account when setting interest rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At the same briefing, \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  Vice Gov. Yi Gang said China remains confident in Europe and will continue to be a long-term investor in the region.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The euro crisis seems to have eased but some issues are still not resolved," Yi said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Our investment portfolio in the euro zone has achieved value protection and enhancement...Such a principle of diversification has proven to be effective," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China will increase its investments in Europe and will further diversify its European assets, Yi said, adding that risk would be the key factor in making such investments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asked about China's investment in Japanese government bonds, Yi said that while in principle the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  seeks to increase investment in the bonds, at a certain point in the past the Japanese had been concerned that Chinese investment had boosted the value of the yen, so China had reduced its investment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Commenting on the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Yi said China has made progress in rebalancing its economy, and it is up to the market to decide whether the yuan is reasonably valued.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China has seen two-way movements and two-way expectations regarding the yuan since late 2011, Yi said, adding the yuan exchange rate will be increasingly market-based.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Zhou also said market supply and demand are playing an increasingly large role in determining the exchange rate, and that as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  approaches an equilibrium exchange rate, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will gradually \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  less in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Referring to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's trade deficit in the first two months of the year, Zhou said the market shouldn't judge China's trade balance based only on short-term data.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Separately, \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  Vice Gov. Hu Xiaolian said at the same briefing that China would move forward with interest-rate reform during its 12th five-year period, which runs from 2011 to 2015.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, Hu said certain conditions, such as the establishment of a system for deposit insurance, must be met before China can go ahead with interest-rate liberalization.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's deposit interest rates are generally positive and only turn negative occasionally, when inflation is high over a short period, Zhou said, adding that higher interest rates could cause hot money inflows.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Aaron Back and Shen Hong, Dow Jones Newswires; (8610) 8400-7701; aaron.back@dowjones.com [ 12-03-12 0517GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120312e83c0007l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
