\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Taiwan c.bank says hot money returning to markets\b0\par\par\uc2 398 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2012\par 03:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds comment)\par \par \uc2 TAIPEI, March 12 (Reuters) - Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor said on Monday that speculative foreign money started returning to Taiwan at the beginning of the year and is continuing to arrive, with money not yet invested in stocks at a higher than healthy level.\par \par \uc2 Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has long taken a strong stance against what it sees as destabilising "hot money" flows, repeatedly warning foreign investors that they must use money brought to the island for stock trading and not leave it uninvested or parked in other assets.\par \par \uc2 In response to questions from lawmakers at a parliamentary hearing, Governor Perng Fai-nan said that some T$170 billion ($5.8 billion) of foreign money had yet to be invested in stocks, but a level of T$120 billion would be "healthy."\par \par \uc2 "There are about 6,000 foreign short-term investment accounts in Taiwan, but only about 20 are actively trading and most of the money is buying stocks," Perng said.\par \par \uc2 "This money is like a big fish jumping into a small pond, creating volatility in the economy and financial markets."\par \par \uc2 Foreign investors were net buyers of Taiwan stocks in December, January and February..\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is keen to protect export-dependent economy from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility and to prevent speculative money from creating asset bubbles. It frequently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 In response to a question on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Perng said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  looked at historical movements in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and referenced moves in the euro, the yen, China's yuan and especially the Korean won when deciding on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Perng also told legislators that he hoped Taiwan and \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  could sign a deal in the near future on a clearing system for China's yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a step towards helping Taiwan grab a part in the internationalisation of the yuan.\par \par \uc2 "We've been talking closely since 2010, but it's sensitive," he said. "It needs a high level decision on the other side."\par \par \uc2 China is already the biggest export destination for Taiwan's export-driven economy, but financial sector ties have not kept pace with growth in manufacturing trade. ($1 = 29.4895 Taiwan dollars)\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Jeanny Kao; Editing by Chris Lewis and Jonathan Standing)\par \par \uc2 TAIWAN-ECONOMY/CBANK  (UPDATE 1) | ABX | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | Z | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120312e83c0002u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
