\par\par \b \uc2 Treasury Bond Market Unscathed By China's Reduced Buying\b0\par\par\uc2 1,121 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Januar 2012\par 13:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- Data suggest a halt in China's buying, if not net selling, of U.S. Treasurys\par \par \uc2 -- But yields have held near record lows.\par \par \uc2 -- Offsets by the \uc2 Fed\uc2  and Japan\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Min Zeng \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Who's afraid of China? Not the Treasurys market, it turns out.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In recent years, as the biggest foreign buyer of U.S. government bonds expanded its holdings at an unprecedented rate, doomsayers warned that the U.S. was bound for a nasty shock if and when Beijing put a brake on that flow of money. Interest rates would surge and the dollar would fall, they said, while the U.S. government would have to fish for alternative buyers of its $15 trillion in debt.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the first real test of that prediction isn't playing out so horribly. Although official U.S. data suggest that China's holdings of U.S. Treasurys shrank in the last quarter as it continued a yearlong process of diversifying its foreign reserves into other countries' assets, Treasury yields, which move inversely to prices, fell. After hitting a recent peak of 2.42% in October, the benchmark 10-year note's yield dropped to 1.876% on Dec. 31.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Wednesday, the Treasury Department reported that Chinese holdings of U.S. Treasury securities dropped $1.5 billion in November to $1.133 trillion after falling by $14.2 billion in October. Skeptics point out that these monthly numbers likely understate the activity from China because its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and sovereign wealth funds also purchase Treasurys via proxies, especially in the U.K.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But even assuming that some of the $7.8 billion increase in U.K. holdings recorded over that same two-month period were from official Chinese sources, the data at least suggest a halt in its buying if not net selling. And with reports from the People's Bank of China showing a third consecutive month of dollar outflows from China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, along with the first quarterly decline since 1998 in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's foreign reserves--to $3.18 trillion--there are reasons to believe that China again failed to add to its Treasurys portfolio last month.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The fear of China is overblown," said Eric Green, chief U.S. rates strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 TD Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York. "If China buys [no Treasurys] then it could push rates marginally higher over the near term, but the market will find equilibrium with higher domestic savings and lower budget deficits."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In fact, if all other factors were held equal, last year's slowdown in Chinese purchases would have driven yields higher since the outstanding stock of Treasury debt continued to rise by around $300 billion in every quarter last year. One reason that didn't happen is because two big buyers of U.S. government bonds stepped up to fill China's shoes: the U.S. Federal Reserve and Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's Treasury holdings fell by $27.5 billion over the past year through November, a number easily offset by a $156.6 billion increase from Japan, which was paid for mainly by the dollar proceeds of the Bank of Japan's yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support exports.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  remains the biggest foreign holder of U.S. Treasurys, Japan's buying has seen that country's holdings rise over the $1 trillion mark, putting it within $100 billion of its Asian neighbor's tally. \f28 \uc2 \f2 The Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which sought to boost the U.S. economy by buying bonds via its "quantitative easing" programs and "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Twist" maturity-extension policies, has risen to become the biggest holder, with a portfolio worth about $1.55 trillion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, both foreign and domestic private sector investors have increased their purchases of Treasurys as they've sought a safe harbor from the euro-zone debt crisis and American households have increased their savings, driving a bigger flow of domestic money into U.S. Treasurys.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 All this buying has helped keep Treasury yields near historically low levels, with the 10-year yield trading at 1.973% Thursday, only about 30 basis points higher than the historic low of 1.672% hit in September. That has put an anchor on private sector interest rates charged to U.S. consumers and businesses, while the inflow of capital has in recent months boosted the dollar against most of its counterparts--the opposite of what the China threat was said to produce.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ward McCarthy, chief financial economist within the fixed income group at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jefferies & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., notes that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  is committed to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Twist's long-dated bond purchases through June and will probably start another round of quantitative easing later this year, which would muffle any noticeable rise in yields.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If China abstains from buying while the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Japan and other buyers pull back, yields would quite likely rise. But whereas the threat of a big China pullout is an outside shock that would rock the U.S. economy, a withdrawal by those actors would likely signal a more welcome pickup in risk appetite for other securities as both investors and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  dialed up their expectations for the global economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In any case, China is not expected to take drastic action. Even now, last year's selling was concentrated in low-yielding short-dated Treasurys known as T-bills while it increased its holdings of notes and bonds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Economists say China needs to continue buying Treasurys, albeit at a slower pace, as its large trade surplus demands that it recycle dollars into the Treasurys market. What's more, it has few incentives to dump its holdings en masse because any sharp rise in yields would not only hurt the value of its holdings but do damage to the U.S. economy, the export market on which China's own economic growth depends.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There is no way China will wake up and suddenly dump Treasurys because it poses as much risk to China as to the U.S.," said Kathy Lien, director of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  research at GFT.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Min Zeng writes about global fixed income and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets for Dow Jones Newswires. He can be reached at 212-416-2229 or via email at: min.zeng@dowjones.com; @djfxtrader)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAmericas@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.) [ 01-20-12 0736ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020120120e81k0003y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\p