\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ UPDATE: China Yuan Push A 'Disguise' For Capital-Account Reform - Former PBOC Adviser\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bob Davis   \par \uc2 Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL   \par \uc2 545 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Januar 2012\par 07:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BEIJING (Dow Jones)--Former People's Bank of China adviser Yu Yongding said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's efforts to boost the yuan's use overseas had become a "disguise" for pushing capital account liberalization, even though the country hasn't taken necessary steps to allow the free flow of capital.\par \par \uc2 "Capital account liberalization has been pushed in a stealth way" through the yuan internationalization program by the People's Bank of China, the influential economist told The Wall Street Journal. While the free flow of capital is on the agenda of many reformers, Mr. Yu--a former member of \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's advisory committee--said other measures should be put in place first.\par \par \uc2 "If we concentrate too much on renminbi internationalization, we could miss the point," said Mr. Yu, referring to the yuan by its other name. Before the capital account is broadly opened, China should allow interest rates and exchange rates to be set by markets, not tightly controlled by the government, he argued\par \par \uc2 "If capital flows freely and you don't have flexible interest rates, as the Asian financial crisis shows, a shock [from external events] could be serious," Mr. Yu said. "It would be difficult to fend off speculative attacks."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Yu said that the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's efforts to give the yuan a bigger international role haven't amounted to much. Depending on where the dollar is priced more cheaply, Chinese trading firms either buy U.S. dollars in Hong Kong or Shanghai to conduct trade. "But trade is mostly still invoiced in dollars; you still need dollars," whether the dollars are purchased in one Chinese city or another, he said.\par \par \uc2 The economist has called for China to let its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  float, so long as that move is tied to capital controls so the country would be protected against a sudden surge or withdrawal of investment. As a back-up plan, he said, China should increase the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading band, from 0.5% on either side of the rate fixed by the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  daily to 1% or more.\par \par \uc2 Over time "the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would appreciate" because China is likely to run a trade surplus, though with a much smaller magnitude, he forecast. "But there would be two-way fluctuation," he said.\par \par \uc2 The latter is important because it would discourage speculation on the yuan. "It's a good time for China to be more flexible," Mr. Yu said. "I don't think the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  actively in either direction."\par \par \uc2 He also criticized U.S. politicians for continuing to press Beijing to appreciate the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rather than let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  float, even if that means that the yuan falls against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 "Americans shouldn't continue to force renminbi appreciation," he said. "The key is to allow the market rate to decide the value of the renminbi."\par \par \uc2 That would allow market forces--rather than government orders--to allocate capital and produce "a natural rebalancing," of the economy, Mr. Yu said, so China wouldn't rely so much on investment and exports for growth.\par \par \uc2 -By Bob Davis, The Wall Street Journal; Bob.Davis@wsj.com [ 18-01-12 0621GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120118e81i000br\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscale