\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Euro Loses Further Prop As  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Reserves Shrink\b0\par\par\uc2 658 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Januar 2012\par 15:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 -- Central banks reel in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 -- \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserves shrank to the lowest level since the Asian crisis\par \par \uc2 -- Euro will come under heavy pressure as a consequence, driving it lower against the dollar \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Alexandra Fletcher \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--The euro-zone debt crisis grinds on, the region's economy is creaking and now the common \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  seems to be losing another crucial prop: support from foreign central banks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Typically, Asian central banks buy euros in large volumes as they diversify the dollars they purchase to weaken their own \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on a regular basis. But that crutch is now wobbling, giving euro bears more confidence in their views.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  volumes are shrinking, and central banks' willingness to buy euros is crumbling. It all adds to the pressure on the euro, which has already fallen by more than 2% against the dollar, and nearly 3% against the yen, so far this year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Reserve data in the second half of 2011 demonstrates a significant weakening in reserve accumulation as compared with previous years," said Jens Nordvig, head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at Nomura Holdings Ltd., in a note to clients Sunday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nomura's analysis of reserves held by 27 central banks shows that the emerging-market buildup in reserves to hold down local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  "was essentially zero" in the fourth quarter of last year, Nordvig said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This shift has already begun to affect global asset prices and is particularly evident in recent moves in the euro against the dollar," he added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China is a particular area of focus here. \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Friday that the country saw its huge \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves shrink in the last few months of 2011, marking the first quarterly drop since the Asian crisis of 1998. Reserves fell by almost $21 billion in the last three months of the year, leaving $3.18 trillion in the bank.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That still leaves China with enormous reserves, and there isn't any clear evidence that it is actively selling euros. If it was, on any meaningful scale, the euro would almost certainly be a good deal weaker by now. Shifting \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  valuations, with the euro falling from $1.40 against the dollar in early September, to under $1.30 by the end of the year, probably account for a chunk of the decline.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, this shift from build-up to stagnation, possibly fueled by growing signs of capital flight from China, is reverberating around the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It isn't just the slower pace of reserve accumulation that poses a risk to the euro. Anecdotal evidence suggests that some reserve managers could be set to shy away from buying euros, too.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Middle East official investors and Asian central banks are likely to start cutting some of their exposures to the euro zone," said Mansoor Mohi-uddin, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at UBS AG, in a note to clients Saturday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Aside from the obvious stresses on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the safer pockets of the region's bond markets are relatively small. "The German bund market, at around $1.5 trillion in size, cannot absorb all the estimated 20%-30% of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves and sovereign wealth funds currently held in euro-zone assets," Mohi-uddin said. "This suggests there will be further selling by official investors of the euro during the first quarter of 2012," he added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Both Nomura and \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  said this shift adds to their confidence in predicting a slide in the euro over the coming months. Nomura expects the euro to trade at $1.20 by the end of this year, while \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  predicts $1.15.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At 1448 GMT Monday, the euro traded at $1.2666 against the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Alexandra Fletcher, Dow Jones Newswires, 44 20 7842 9462; alexandra.fletcher@dowjones.com @djfxtrader\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 January 16, 2012 09:54 ET (14:54 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020120116e81g003vm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
