\par\par \b \uc2 China Sets Yuan Weaker, Surprising Market And Driving Down Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 585 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Januar 2012\par 05:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI (Dow Jones)--\uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  set an unexpectedly weak fixing for the yuan against the U.S. dollar Thursday morning, dragging down \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  throughout the region.\par \par \uc2 The market action shows how the yuan, even with its tightly controlled exchange rate, can set the tone for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading across Asia.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  on Thursday set the dollar/yuan central parity at 6.3115, significantly above Wednesday's 6.3001, which was an all-time low. A higher dollar/yuan fixing means the yuan is weaker against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The euro, Australian dollar, Singapore dollar and other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  ticked down on the move, though they recouped much of their fall within an hour or two.\par \par \uc2 As Asian nations fight to maintain their export competitiveness in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's shadow, central banks often \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  get out of line with regional peers, especially the yuan. Traders aware of such patterns may have taken the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  fixing as a sign that yuan appreciation will be less vigorous going forward, limiting the prospects for other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well.\par \par \uc2 Within China, trading of the yuan against the dollar is limited to a band controlled by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  sets a daily reference rate each morning, with the spot rate permitted to trade no more than 0.5% above or below that point.\par \par \uc2 The dollar rose as high as CNY6.3064 after the fixing, compared to CNY6.2946 late Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Traders said the surprising fixing is a roll-back of the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's overly aggressive--and largely politically motivated--drive to guide the yuan higher in the last days of 2011.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  paved the way for its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hit record highs in spot trading last week, using strong central parity fixings and suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That secured a 4.7% appreciation for the yuan versus the dollar over the full year and signaled to the market that Beijing would continue to allow the yuan to strengthen against the dollar in 2012, traders and analysts said.\par \par \uc2 Thursday's fixing reverses part of that move, consistent with the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's desire to see more two-way fluctuation in the exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  wants to play with the market. They dislike speculation," one \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at a foreign bank in Shanghai said.\par \par \uc2 Traders also said the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's move may have come in response to a weakening euro. The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  sets central parity only against the dollar but it monitors a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and with Europe the largest destination for Chinese exports, a euro trading Thursday near 18-month lows against the yuan was certain to catch the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's attention.\par \par \uc2 Investors for months have viewed the yuan as a sure bet to appreciate, playing into Beijing's ambition to expand the yuan's global importance.\par \par \uc2 However, some of that confidence was shaken in the fourth quarter as traders repeatedly pushed the yuan to the bottom of its daily trading band as China's economy lost some of its luster. Analysts expect the yuan to gain only about 3% against the dollar this year as Western demand for Chinese exports continues to slip.\par \par \uc2 A local-bank trader in Shanghai said "the fixing reaffirmed that the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  is not looking for the dollar to fall below CNY6.30, possibly for the first quarter."\par \par \uc2 -By Jean Yung, Dow Jones Newswires; 8621 6120-1200; jean.yung@dowjones.com [ 05-01-12 0441GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120105e81500068\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
