\par\par \b \uc2 Ex-PBOC Adviser: Yuan Would Not Rise Sharply If PBOC Halts  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 264 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Januar 2012\par 05:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BEIJING (Dow Jones)--The Chinese yuan is not likely to rise significantly, and may even depreciate, if the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  stops \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, a former \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  adviser said Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Yu Yongding, now a senior economist at the influential Chinese Academy of Social Sciences, said that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could consider halting its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market altogether at an "appropriate time" in the future and let the yuan float freely.\par \par \uc2 The yuan is still expected to appreciate over the longer term and China's economy can adjust to this trend, Yu noted in written remarks distributed to reporters.\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been pushing the yuan higher by setting a stronger daily parity rate against the dollar. The market, however, has been trying to push the yuan lower, partly due to near-term concerns over slower growth for the Chinese economy as well as the dollar's continuing attraction as a safe haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  keeps a tight rein on trading, imposing a narrow daily trading range around the parity rate.\par \par \uc2 Yu also said that China should maintain its capital controls to guard against "hot money" inflows and possible risks brought by excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves.\par \par \uc2 "China should step up its capital account restrictions while moving forward with interest rate and exchange rate liberalization later, moving on to capital account liberalization," he said.\par \par \uc2 -By China Bureau, Dow Jones Newswires; (8610) 8400-7799; djnews.beijing@dowjones.com [ 05-01-12 0419GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120105e8150005t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
