\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 6-U.S. says China not  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  manipulator; chides Japan\b0\par\par\uc2 1,208 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Dezember 2011\par 22:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Treasury says yuan appreciation "insufficient"\par \par \uc2 * U.S. to monitor yuan, press for greater flexibility\par \par \uc2 * Report leaves Obama approach unchanged on China \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Manufacturing group disappointed by report\par \par \uc2 * US Treasury criticises Japan's solo \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 (Adds Xinhua commentary)\par \par \uc2 By Pedro Nicolaci da Costa\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON, Dec 27 (Reuters) - The U.S. Treasury again shied away from labeling China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator on Tuesday, but it rapped the country for not moving quickly enough on exchange rate reforms.\par \par \uc2 The United States also chided Japan for stepping into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stem the yen's rise, and urged South Korea to use such \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  sparingly.\par \par \uc2 Some U.S. politicians have argued that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has gained an unfair competitive edge in global markets by keeping the yuan artificially low to boost exports, and pressure has mounted in Congress for President Barack Obama to punish China.\par \par \uc2 But the administration prefers to tread softly and use diplomacy. The U.S. Treasury, in a semi-annual report, as usual said that statutes covering a designation of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator "have not been met with respect to China."\par \par \uc2 It repeated its standard line that appreciation in the yuan has been too slow, calling it "insufficient."\par \par \uc2 "Treasury will closely monitor the pace of appreciation and press for policy changes that yield greater exchange rate flexibility, a level playing field, and a sustained shift to domestic demand-led growth," it said in the report to Congress on international economic and exchange rate policies.\par \par \uc2 The value of the yuan, which Beijing manages closely, has risen 4 percent against the dollar this year and 7.7 percent since China dropped a firm peg against the greenback in June 2010. The Peterson Institute for International Economics recently estimated the yuan was undervalued by 24 percent against the dollar, down from 28 percent earlier in the year. It attributed the change to both Beijing's policy of gradual \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation and higher Chinese inflation.\par \par \uc2 At the heart of the friction between the two countries is a U.S. trade deficit with China that swelled in 2010 to a record $273.1 billion from about $226.9 billion in 2009. The cumulative Jan-Oct deficit with China is on track to top that this year, running at around $245.5 billion.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. Senate this year for the first time passed a bill that would require the administration to slap penalties on Chinese imports if it fails to adopt market-based exchange rates. While the measure has made no progress in the lower chamber and is unlikely to become law, it shows the mounting U.S. frustration with its vital trade partner.\par \par \uc2 President Obama at the November APEC meetings, in his toughest words yet, told President Hu Jintao that China must play by global trade rules and act like "a grown-up."\par \par \uc2 The Treasury's decision not to label China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator sent a "clear and positive signal" that would soothe the market and benefit trade, according to a commentary in Xinhua, the Chinese state news agency, on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Beijing has warned the United States not to "politicize" the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue, and some economists have pointed out that nations such as Japan and Switzerland have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets without drawing Washington's ire.\par \par \uc2 TARGETING TOKYO\par \par \uc2 The report did point the finger at Japan this time, criticizing Tokyo for its solo yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in August and October that followed a joint Group of 7 action in the aftermath of the March 11 earthquake.\par \par \uc2 "The unilateral Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were undertaken when exchange market conditions appeared to be operating in an orderly manner and volatility in the yen-dollar exchange rate was lower than, for example, the euro-dollar market," the report said.\par \par \uc2 "In contrast to the post-earthquake joint G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in March, the United States did not support these \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," the Treasury said, adding that Tokyo should pursue reforms to revive its domestic economy rather than try to influence the exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 A senior Japanese government official said the report did not change Tokyo's position that its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy was in line with G7 agreements.\par \par \uc2 "This report does not make it more difficult for Japan to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said the official, who spoke on condition of anonymity due to the sensitivity of the topic. "We are committed to doing whatever is necessary."\par \par \uc2 Japanese exporters have complained that the ultra-strong yen puts them at a competitive disadvantage. The yen was trading at just under 78 to the U.S. dollar on Wednesday morning, about 3 percent weaker than it was on Oct. 31, when Tokyo aggressively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap the rise.\par \par \uc2 The report also noted that South Korean authorities "should limit their \b \highlight22\uc2 FX interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to exceptional circumstances of disorderly market conditions and adopt a greater degree of exchange rate flexibility."\par \par \uc2 MORE OF THE SAME\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner has said the law on the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  report, which requires the administration to determine whether U.S. trade partners are deliberately undervaluing their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is a poor tool to push Beijing on the yuan.\par \par \uc2 Instead, the United States prefers to argue for change at regular closed-door meetings with Chinese officials. It also uses international economic forums, such as the Group of 20 leading nations and the International Monetary Fund, to ramp up public pressure on Beijing to move more quickly to a more-flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 China is the biggest foreign holder of U.S. Treasuries, with about $1.1 trillion, a position that gives it leverage in international economic negotiations. \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders had not expected a change of U.S. tactics.\par \par \uc2 "It's not very surprising. It's sort of sliding it in under the radar. They're (Treasury) really not in a position to make any major moves at this point," said Sean Incremona, an economist at 4Cast in New York.\par \par \uc2 The Treasury Department has not labeled a country a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator since July 1994, when it cited China. A designation would require the United States to step up negotiations with Beijing on the yuan's value.\par \par \uc2 The yuan slipped on Tuesday as strong dollar demand from corporations offset a record high mid-point fixed by the People's Bank of China. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set an all-time high dollar/yuan mid-point in an apparent move to let the yuan rise a little more at the end of 2011 so as to make the yuan's full-year nominal appreciation look bigger, traders said.\par \par \uc2 Some U.S. manufacturers, which have been hit hardest by competition from China and other emerging economies, would still prefer the U.S. government to take a harder line.\par \par \uc2 "China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still enormously undervalued," said Scott Paul, executive director of the Alliance for American Manufacturing, an industry lobby for hard-hit textile, steel and labor groups.\par \par \uc2 "I'm disappointed that President Obama has now formally refused six times to cite China for its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation, a practice which has contributed to the loss of hundreds of thousands of American manufacturing jobs."\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Luciana Lopez and Doug Palmer, and Stanley White in Tokyo; Editing by Leslie Adler and Dan Grebler)\par \par \uc2 USA-CHINA/\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111230e7cr000uk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
