\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2  Yuan Down Late On Fixing, Dollar Strength; PBOC  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Suspected For Third Straight Session\b0\par\par\uc2 391 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Dezember 2011\par 10:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                  Vs Parity    Pvs \par USD/CNY Central Parity   6.3351              6.3303 \par USD/CNY OTC 0830 GMT     6.3472    +0.19%    6.3378 \par              High        6.3546    +0.31% \par              Low         6.3350    -0.00% \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SHANGHAI (Dow Jones)--China's yuan fell against the U.S. dollar late Tuesday, tracking the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pair's daily reference exchange rate, although traders believe that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for a third straight session to prevent the Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from softening even further.\f28 \par \par \uc2 On the over-the-counter market, the dollar was at CNY6.3472 around 0830 GMT, up from CNY6.3378 late Monday. It traded between CNY6.3350 and CNY6.3546.\par \par \uc2 The yuan is up 3.8% against the dollar this year and 7.6% since June 2010, when China ended its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peg to the dollar and vowed to make the yuan more flexible.\par \par \uc2 Ahead of trading, the People's Bank of China fixed the dollar/yuan central parity rate at 6.3351, up from Monday's 6.3303, on the back of a broadly stronger dollar overseas.\par \par \uc2 Traders said the PBOC may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yuan for a third straight session.\par \par \uc2 "The trend appears to be that the PBOC will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the greenback rises above the CNY6.35 mark," a Shanghai-based trader said. China is still intent to let the yuan appreciate further before year-end to possibly around CNY6.3000 against the greenback, the trader said.\par \par \uc2 "These \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market are in line with official rhetoric to keep the yuan stable," said a local bank trader in Beijing.\par \par \uc2 Offshore, the yuan rose against the dollar in both the nondeliverable forward and spot markets on corporate demand for the yuan offshore. One-year dollar-yuan nondeliverable forward contracts fell to 6.3960/6.4010 from 6.4150/6.4190 late Monday in Asia, and implying a 0.8% fall in the yuan against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the next year.\par \par \uc2 In the offshore yuan market in Hong Kong, where the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  floats freely, the dollar-yuan exchange rate was at 6.3640 late Tuesday, down from 6.3775 late Monday.\par \par \uc2 -By Jean Yung, Dow Jones Newswires; 8621 6120-1200; jean.yung@dowjones.com [ 20-12-11 0917GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1153   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111220e7ck000hx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
