\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 4- \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2 's yuan jumps on signs of c.bank  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 902 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Dezember 2011\par 03:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * State banks seen offering large amounts of dollars-traders\par \par \uc2 * C.bank has been setting higher mid-points as spot hit limit down\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 PBOC\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support yuan extremely rare\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 PBOC\uc2  could re-peg yuan to dollar but lets it move in wider range\par \par \uc2 (Updates with close)\par \par \uc2 By Lu Jianxin and Kazunori Takada\par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, Dec 16 (Reuters) - The yuan jumped to a record high on Friday against the dollar on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  orchestrated by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , its most explicit action in three months to deter speculators from betting on a fall in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \uc2 offered large amounts of dollars via major state banks, propelling the yuan to a record high of 6.3294 per dollar, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said.\par \par \uc2 The yuan pulled back slightly by the close to trade at 6.3484, but was still up sharply from Thursday's close of 6.3735.\par \par \uc2 "This is a clear \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the government to support the yuan and is in line with the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's recent moves to use the mid-point to prevent the yuan's fall," said a trader at a European bank in Shanghai.\par \par \uc2 "The move indicates that the government is determined to maintain the stability of the yuan's value in the near term, possibly even let it appreciate slightly."\par \par \uc2 Weakening export demand and worries about how China will weather a housing market slowdown have cast doubt on 2012 economic prospects. Economists widely expect China's growth next year will pull back to the slowest pace in a decade.\par \par \uc2 That has put added pressure on the yuan.\par \par \uc2 Since late September, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been trying to deter bets for yuan depreciation by setting the daily dollar/yuan mid-point, which determines the starting point for trade, at stronger yuan levels.\par \par \uc2 The suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday, an ultra-rare example of the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  visibly supporting the yuan, seemed to achieve Beijing's goal. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was likely to stabilise between 6.30 to 6.40 at least for the rest of this year, traders said.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  fixed the mid-point at 6.3352 to the dollar, stronger than 6.3421 on Thursday. Trading of the yuan is limited to 0.5 percent either side of the daily mid-point.\par \par \uc2 Despite the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's attempts to set the yuan's exchange rate higher via the mid-point, a shortage of dollars in the onshore China market and broad dollar strength in global markets have pushed the yuan down to the weaker end of its band nearly every day in December.\par \par \uc2 That was an unusual bout of weakness for a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that has largely been on a rising trend since a landmark revaluation in 2005. The yuan is up around 30 percent since the revaluation.\par \par \uc2 REVERSE OF TREND\par \par \uc2 For several years, the yuan has been considered by investors as a sure-fire appreciation bet as China amassed large surpluses from trade and Beijing came under pressure internationally to let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  has acted to cushion speculation of yuan appreciation from time to time since the 2005 revaluation but has rarely needed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But some investors are now speculating in the offshore forward markets that the yuan will fall in the near-term as China's exports are buffeted by a weak global economy.\par \par \uc2 "I'm not too surprised that the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  is taking it (dollar) a little lower ahead of year-end," said Andy Ji, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2  in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 "USD/CNY is down by 4 percent year-to-date and seems that the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  is committed to keep that pace of appreciation even with deterioration in the external environment."\par \par \uc2 Ren Xianfang, senior China economist at IHS Global Insight, said the downward pressure on the yuan was part of a global move by investors into dollar assets, triggered by debt restructuring risks in Europe and a consequent dollar shortage in the international financial system.\par \par \uc2 "This is a global problem that is now being reflected in emerging markets worldwide. September was basically a turning point for capital flow for emerging markets," she said, adding that there would likely be a major restructuring in the first quarter of next year and that will make the flight to dollars even more aggressive globally.\par \par \uc2 Beijing seems wary of sharp swings amid weak external demand and financial market turmoil.\par \par \uc2 Over the past few weeks, it has leashed the yuan within a tight range around 6.35 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 During the 2008 financial crisis, it put the yuan mainly in a range of around 6.80 to 6.85 against the dollar and kept it there for about two years.\par \par \uc2 A dealer said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had had the impact Beijing desired.\par \par \uc2 "Worries over yuan depreciation dispersed quickly, with the market now abundant with dollar liquidity," said the dealer at an Asian bank in Shanghai.\par \par \uc2 "The yuan is now expected to move mainly around 6.35 in the near term, possibly hitting a high of around 6.30 at the end of this year," he said, echoing the views of many traders.\par \par \uc2 (Additional Reporting by Chen Yixin, and Nick Edwards and Kevin Yao in Beijing; Editing by Neil Fullick and Richard Borsuk)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-CHINA-YUAN/JUMP (UPDATE 4)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111219e7cg0003f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
