\par\par \b \uc2 DJ China Shares End Sharply Higher On Hopes Of Government Support -3-\b0\par\par\uc2 493 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Dezember 2011\par 09:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI (Dow Jones)--China's shares rose sharply Friday, ending a six-day losing streak because of hopes Beijing would take measures to prop up stock prices, while \uc2 New China Life Insurance\uc2 's stronger-than-expected debut also lent support.\par \par \uc2 The Shanghai Composite Index, which tracks both A and B shares, ended up 2.0%, or 43.94 points, at 2224.84, following a 6.5% cumulative loss in the past six sessions. The Shenzhen Composite Index rose 2.8%, or 24.34 points, to 910.35.\par \par \uc2 Despite the gains, analysts remain cautious about the market, saying further rises would likely be capped by the usual tight liquidity near year-end and lingering concerns over China's economic slowdown.\par \par \uc2 Sentiment was boosted by comments from Guo Shuqing, chief of China's securities regulator, that the government could consider letting more local pension and housing funds invest in its capital markets, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 "Although Guo's suggestion is unlikely to lead to anything in the short term, his remarks indicate the market's recent tumble has caught the attention of the government," said Jiang Shiqin, an analyst at Industrial Securities.\par \par \uc2 "China's financial market is still a policy-driven sector. So, it's widely believed that if share prices continues to drop, Beijing will have to do something to stabilize the market," he said.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected by traders of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yuan after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent slump spooked investors.\par \par \uc2 Financial firms were among the day's biggest gainers, led by \uc2 New China Life Insurance\uc2 , which ended up 14% at CNY26.44 on its first day of trading in Shanghai. Analysts had expected the fourth-largest life insurer on the mainland to trade around its initial public offering price of CNY23.25.\par \par \uc2 They attributed the stronger-than-expected performance to expectations \uc2 New China Life\uc2  can increase premiums at a faster-than-average rate in the coming years.\par \par \uc2 The insurer has long suffered from a capital shortage. With the $1.9 billion proceeds raised from its dual IPO, "it's expected to post annual compound growth of 24% in its net profit over the next few years," said China Merchants Securities analyst Hong Jinping.\par \par \uc2 China Life was up 1.4% at CNY16.84, Ping An gained 2.1% to CNY35.95 and China Pacific rose 2.2% to CNY18.77.\par \par \uc2 Solar-panel makers also gained after China on Thursday issued long-awaited guidelines for the renewable energy industry. The country has targeted 15 gigawatts of solar-power capacity by the end of 2015.\par \par \uc2 Sunvim Group\uc2  rose 3.2% to CNY5.16, Beijing Jingyuntong was up 4.4% at CNY24.36 and Jiangsu Sunshine increased 2.3% to CNY3.12.\par \par \uc2 -Rose Yu contributed to this article, \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\uc2 ; 8621 6120-1200; rose.yu@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020111216e7cg003ml\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
