\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Update\b0\par\par\uc2 1,623 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Dezember 2011\par 01:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 US Dollar\par \par \uc2 The euro fell to its lowest level in more than a week before retracing slightly on Thursday as the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy moves were not seen as comprehensive enough. Late Thursday, the euro was at $1.3339 from $1.3413 late Wednesday, according to EBS via CQG. The common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at Y103.59 from Y104.17. The dollar was at Y77.63 from Y77.68 and at CHF0.9259 from CHF0.9235. The U.K. pound was at $1.5640 from $1.5708. The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was at about 78.828 from about 78.485.\par \par \uc2 Euro\par \par \uc2 EUR/USD - to consolidate with weak tone ahead of conclusion of EU leaders summit in Brussels. Euro sentiment hurt after ECB yesterday cut its key lending rate to 1% from 1.25% as widely expected but Draghi downplayed idea of more aggressive bond-buys to help support stressed euro-zone debt markets; Germany opposed ideas in EU draft, seen by Dow Jones, about granting ESM a banking license or having EFSF overlap the ESM; European Banking Authority saying German banks had capital shortfall of EUR13.1 billion, nearly triple the result of a previous test in October; S&P placing 15 Spanish banks on watch for downgrade. EUR/USD also weighed by fears of euro-zone recession. But EUR/USD losses tempered by sovereign demand for euro; positions adjustment before weekend. EUR/USD daily chart mixed as MACD bullish, but stochastics turning bearish; bearish outside-day-range pattern completed yesterday. Support at 1.3289 (yesterday's low); breach would expose downside to 1.3259 (Nov. 30 low), then 1.3243 (Nov. 28 low), 1.3212 (Nov. 25 low) and 1.3145 (Oct. 4 swing low). Resistance at 1.3375 (hourly chart), then at 1.3460 (yesterday's high) and 1.3486 (Monday's high); breach would target 1.3550 (Dec. 2 high), then 1.3569 (Nov. 22 high), 1.3595 (55-day moving average), 1.3615 (Nov. 18 high) and 1.3641 (Nov. 15 high).\par \par \uc2 Japanese Yen\par \par \uc2 USD/JPY - to consolidate as markets await outcome of European leaders summit to resolve the euro-zone sovereign-debt crisis. USD/JPY undermined by capital flows to safe-haven yen and unwinding of yen-funded carry trades as risk aversion increases after rocky start to EU summit yesterday and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined to enact new bond purchases; German resistance to changes such as allowing two bailout funds heightened skepticism that the Brussels summit can produce solution to sovereign-debt troubles. USD/JPY also weighed by Japan exporter sales. But investor sentiment soothed by bigger-than-expected 23,000 drop in latest U.S. weekly jobless claims, while U.S. wholesale inventories for October rose 1.6% as companies stocked up in anticipation of strong holiday sales. USD/JPY downside also limited by Japan importer demand, expectations of Japan MOF's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; positions adjustment before weekend. Yen crosses vulnerable to \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  November CPI, PPI, fixed assets investment, retail sales, industrial output data. USD/JPY daily chart mixed as MACD in bullish mode, bullish doji-hammer candlestick pattern completed yesterday; but stochastics bearish, while 5-day moving average meandering sideways. Resistance at 77.80-77.86 band (yesterday's high-Tuesday's high), then at 78.11-78.15 band (Monday's high-Nov. 30 high) and 78.29 (Nov. 29 high); breach would target 78.41 (Nov. 2 high), then 79.10 (Nov. 1 high) and 79.55 (Oct. 31 high). Support at 77.13 (yesterday's low, matching Nov. 25 low and confluence of 55-day and 100 day moving averages); breach would target 77.00-76.92 band (Nov. 24 low-Nov. 23 low), then 76.83-76.73 band (Nov. 22 low-Nov. 21 low) and 76.57 (Nov. 18 low).\par \par \uc2 Sterling\par \par \uc2 GBP/USD - to consolidate with weak bias as markets await outcome of EU leaders summit. No \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reaction to BOE's widely expected decision yesterday to leave its interest rates unchanged and maintained size of its asset purchases at GBP275 billion. GBP/USD undermined by heightened investor risk aversion; expectations of further quantitative easing from BOE in February. But GBP/USD losses tempered by positions adjustment before weekend. Data focus: 0930 GMT UK October global goods trade balance, October CML regulated mortgage survey, November producer prices. Daily chart mixed as MACD & stochastics in bullish mode; but bearish dark-cloud-cover candlestick pattern completed yesterday. Support at 1.5609 (yesterday's low), then at 1.5593 (Wednesday's low); breach would target 1.5559 (Tuesday's low), then 1.5524 (Nov. 30 low), 1.5466-1.5456 band (Nov. 29 low-Nov. 28 low) and 1.5421 (Nov. 25 low). Resistance at 1.5671 (hourly chart), then at 1.5708 (hourly chart) and 1.5770 (yesterday's high); breach would target 1.5796 (Nov. 21 high), then 1.5888 (Nov. 18 high) and 1.5931 (Nov. 15 high).\par \par \uc2 Swiss Franc\par \par \uc2 USD/CHF - to trade with firm tone ahead of ahead of conclusion of EU leaders summit. USD/CHF buoyed by broadly firmer USD undertone as investor risk aversion stays elevated; expectations of \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's CHF-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But USD/CHF gains tempered by positions adjustment before weekend. Daily chart mixed as stochastics in bullish mode, bullish outside-day-range pattern completed yesterday; but MACD still bearish. Resistance at 0.9290-0.9294 band (yesterday's high-Tuesday's high); breach would target 0.9306 (Nov. 28 high), then 0.9330 (Nov. 25 high), 0.9340 (April 1 reaction high), 0.9369 (March 9 reaction high) and 0.9505 (Feb. 22 high). Support at 0.9219 (hourly chart), then at 0.9174 (yesterday's low) and 0.9161 (Monday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.9108 (Dec. 2 low), then 0.9068-0.9061 band (Dec. 1 low-Nov. 30 low), 0.9048 (55-day moving average) and 0.8958-0.8950 band (Nov. 14 low-Nov. 11 low).\par \par \uc2 Canadian Dollar\par \par \uc2 USD/CAD - to trade with bullish bias as markets await outcome of EU leaders summit. USD/CAD underpinned by increased investor risk aversion, softer commodity & oil prices (Nymex crude settled down $2.15 yesterday at $98.34/bbl. But USD/CAD gains tempered by positions adjustment before weekend. Data focus: 1330 GMT Canada October international merchandise trade. USD/CAD daily chart mixed as MACD bearish, but stochastics turned bullish at oversold; bullish outside-day-range pattern completed yesterday. Resistance at 1.0286 (hourly chart); breach would expose upside to 1.0343 (hourly chart), then 1.0363 (Nov. 29 high), 1.0468 (Nov. 28 high) and 1.0523 (Nov. 25 high). Support at 1.0178 (hourly chart), then at 1.0121 (hourly chart) and 1.0049 (yesterday's low, near 100-day moving average); breach would target 0.9970 (Nov. 1 low), then 0.9888 (Oct. 27 swing low).\par \par \uc2 Australian Dollar\par \par \uc2 AUD/USD to consolidate with soft tone ahead of outcome of EU leaders summit. AUD/USD undermined by intensified investor risk aversion; softer commodity prices. But AUD/USD losses tempered by AUD-USD interest-rate differential, sovereign demand for Aussie, positions adjustment before weekend. AUD/USD vulnerable to slew of China November data. Daily chart mixed as MACD bullish, but stochastics turned bearish at overbought; bearish outside-day-range pattern completed yesterday. Support at 1.0142 (yesterday's low), then at 1.0111 (55-day moving average); breach would expose downside to 0.9938 (Nov. 30 low), then 0.9859 (Nov. 29 low) and 0.9753 (Nov. 28 low). Resistance at 1.0231 (hourly chart), then at 1.0285 (hourly chart) and 1.0378 (yesterday's high); breach would target 1.0412 (200-day moving average), then 1.0446 (Nov. 3 high) and 1.0566 (Nov. 1 high).\par \par \uc2 New Zealand Dollar\par \par \uc2 NZD/USD to consolidate with weak tone as markets await outcome of EU leaders summit. NZD/USD undermined by strong investor risk aversion, softer commodity prices. But NZD/USD losses tempered by NZD-USD yield gap; positions adjustment before weekend. Daily chart mixed as MACD bullish, but stochastics turned bearish at overbought; bearish outside-day-range pattern completed yesterday. Support at 0.7706 (yesterday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.7572 (Nov. 30 low), then 0.7506 (Nov. 29 low) and 0.7434 (Nov. 28 low). Resistance at 0.7771 (hourly chart), then at 0.7812 (hourly chart) and 0.7877 (yesterday's high); breach would target 0.7930 (Nov. 14 high), then 0.7997 (Nov. 7 reaction high, matching 200-day moving average) and 0.8044 (100-day moving average).\par \par \uc2 China Yuan\par \par \uc2 China's yuan rose a tad against the U.S. dollar late Thursday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  guided the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher via a reference exchange rate. On the over-the-counter market, the dollar was at CNY6.3619 around 0830 GMT, down from Wednesday's CNY6.3643. It traded between CNY6.3553 and the day's upper limit of CNY6.3636 Thursday. Ahead of trading, the People's Bank of China fixed the dollar/yuan central parity rate at 6.3319, compared with Wednesday's 6.3342, tracking a weaker overnight dollar index in New York. Offshore, one-year dollar-yuan nondeliverable forward contracts rose to 6.3915/6.3945 compared with 6.3770/6.3820 late Wednesday. This implies a 0.5% fall in the yuan against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the next year. In Hong Kong, where the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  floats freely, the dollar-yuan exchange rate was at CNY6.3655 late Thursday, down from 6.3705 late Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020111209e7c9000b6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
