\par\par \b \uc2 DJ UPDATE: Ex-PBOC Adviser: Should Hold Off On New Yuan Internationalization Policies\b0\par\par\uc2 458 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Dezember 2011\par 03:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 -- Ex-PBOC adviser Yu Yongding says China should hold off on new yuan internationalization policies amid a weakening yuan\par \par \uc2 -- Yu says Beijing should step up capital account controls to defend against disruptive capital inflows and outflows\par \par \uc2 -- \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and China's growth outlook point to further yuan softening with China recording its first net outflow of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  in October\par \par \uc2 (Adds details from Yu's commentary in the 2nd-3rd, 5th, and 9th paragraphs, background and context throughout) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Jean Yung \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SHANGHAI (Dow Jones)--China should hold off on launching new yuan internationalization policies amid a recent fall in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's value, former \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  adviser Yu Yongding said Monday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In a commentary published in the China Business News, Yu urged the authorities to do just the opposite. Beijing should introduce more capital account controls to bolster defenses against disruptive flows of money entering and exiting China from volatile global financial markets, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Neither the international nor domestic conditions are currently suitable for liberalizing the capital account, or doing so under the name of internationalizing the yuan," Yu wrote.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Since late September, a free-floating yuan in Hong Kong has mostly traded at a discount to its onshore counterpart, while the offshore nondeliverable forward market has been pricing in expectations that the yuan would depreciate. China's financial system also saw a net outflow of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in October for the first this year. The outflow was also the biggest since December 2007.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The recent depreciation trend in the yuan exchange rate, though unlikely to last, is a warning on China's economic outlook, said Yu, now a scholar at the Chinese Academy of Social Sciences.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With Europe perhaps heading into recession, the U.S. struggling with a fragile recovery and Japan battered, China's export growth has been flagging, with expectations of worse to come.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As China's growth declines, so does confidence in the Chinese economy and in the yuan's continued appreciation. Foreign investors will likely pull money out of the country at a rapid rate.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We should take advantage of the current two-way volatility in the yuan exchange rate as an opportunity to reform the exchange rate formation mechanism," Yu said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If the People's Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ceases to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, the yuan may stop appreciating and even depreciate, Yu said. "Even if that happens, the U.S. will have nothing to say because we aren't 'manipulating' (the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 )," he added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Jean Yung, Dow Jones Newswires; 8621 6120-1200; jean.yung@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 December 04, 2011 21:41 ET (02:41 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020111205e7c5000kd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw18