\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: Crude May Push Higher This Week - Ritterbusch\b0\par\par\uc2 1,572 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 November 2011\par 23:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2241 GMT [Dow Jones] Crude is capable of furthering this week's advances for a couple more sessions, with the expected assistance of some more positive U.S. economic news, says Jim Ritterbusch, president of Ritterbusch & Associates. "We have temporarily shifted off of a bearish posture in favor of some additional expected upside follow-through to this month's highs at the $103.37 area per nearby WTI (West Texas Intermediate crude)," Ritterbusch says. Crude futures rose for a fourth session Wednesday after central banks coordinated moves to shore up the global financial system. Nymex crude settled 57 cents higher at $100.36/bbl. But Brent crude settled 30 cents lower at $110.52/bbl and Ritterbusch says Libyan output levels appear to running at least a month ahead of prior expectations. Nymex crude is up 14 cents at $100.50/bbl in Asian trade. (Ross.Kelly@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2236 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's S&P/ASX 200 may rise 3% after the Federal Reserve coordinated a move by central banks to free up funding to financial markets, according to IG Markets. The Fed's move was coordinated with the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the central banks of Canada, Switzerland, Japan and the U.K. and pushed the Dow Jones Industrial Average 4.2% higher to close at 12046 points. At the same time, China cut banks' required reserve ratio 50 basis points. "We expect to see strong gains across the resources and financials along with other cyclical plays that have been beaten down recently. Locally, today's session brings building approvals and retail sales data. Traders will also be looking out for China's manufacturing PMI number, as well as \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's final manufacturing PMI number," said IG Markets in a note to investors. The Australian dollar surged to trade at US$1.0270, adding 3% against the U.S. dollar in the last 24 hours and nearly 5% this week. (andrew.critchlow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 5:10 (Dow Jones) Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut its benchmark interest rate a half-point Wednesday to 11% to help prop up business confidence in the economy amid growing concern about the global economic slowdown, said Andre Perfeito, an economist at Gradual Investimentos in Sao Paulo. The situation abroad is already affecting Brazil's economy by reducing demand for exports, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to encourage Brazilian businesses to continue investing, said Perfeito, who expects half-point rate cuts at each of the next two monetary policy committee meetings, in January and March. (jeffrey.lewis@dowjones.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Call us at (212) 416-3100 or email bradley.davis@dowjones.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 5:07 (Dow Jones) Brazil cuts rates by 50 basis points as expected, highlighting the continued easing trend in emerging market monetary policy as growth expectations scale back. The Brazilian real will likely stay on investors' radars despite the cut, given the country still has sky-high rates compared to both other developed and emerging markets. (erin.mccarthy@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Call us at (212) 416-3100 or email bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2200 GMT [Dow Jones] China's official PMI, due on Thursday, likely fell to 49.7 in November from 50.4 in October, according to the median forecast from a Dow Jones poll of eight economists. As the first contractionary reading below 50 since the aftermath of the global financial crisis in early 2009, it would add to other data indicating China's economy is loosing momentum and would likely prompt a negative market reaction. Analysts say a weak PMI reading would increase the possibility Beijing would need to ease monetary policy sooner than expected to offset the impact of a sluggish global economy. The official PMI data is due at 0100 GMT. (liyan.qi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2200 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Indonesia's trade surplus likely shrank to $2.15 billion in October from $2.72 billion in September as weaker global demand took its toll on the country's exports, according to the median forecast of 10 regional economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires. "It looks like the global economic slowdown has brought prices of many commodities down and economic growth in some of Indonesia's trading partners to slow as well," says Bank Danamon's economist Anton Hendranata. The Central Statistics Agency will release trade data around 0400 GMT. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2200 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Australian October retail sales and building approvals are both expected to have increased, according to a survey of economists by Dow Jones Newswires. The median result of a forecast from 19 economists showed that October retail sales likely rose 0.4% on-month vs September's 0.4% increase, with building approvals likely up 3.0% vs September's 12.0% fall. Both reports are due 0130 GMT. "While we are now unlikely to see a severe confidence-related drop-off in total retail sales, we still expect (October) to show a fairly subdued picture..." say economists at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2158 GMT [Dow Jones] New Zealand's 3Q Terms of Trade data show the impact that a strong New Zealand dollar is having on the country's all important exports sector. Statistics New Zealand says the merchandise terms of trade index fell 0.7% from the second quarter, worse than the median expectation from a Dow Jones Newswires poll of 10 economists that the index would remain flat. After dairy prices boosted the index for several consecutive quarters, dairy export prices for the sector were down 4.8%. "In the September 2011-quarter, the New Zealand dollar appreciated against most of our major trading partner \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . This had a downward impact on both export and import prices," says Statistics New Zealand. Export prices in total were down 4.0% while export volumes fell 0.7%. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2151 GMT (Dow Jones) EUR/USD is likely to continue gaining in the short term as markets are cheered by efforts from major central banks to shore up the global financial system, but Europe's sovereign debt issues remain, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 HiFX\f28 \uc2 \f2  Senior Trader Stuart Ive. "The issues haven't gone away. All that's happening is the financial markets have a reprieve and can function." He adds, however, "apart from an actual default or an actual country saying we are leaving (the euro zone)," it will be very hard to sell into this environment. The pair is at 1.3438 and he says support is at 1.3400 while resistance is at 1.3530. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Call us at (212) 416-3100 or email bradley.davis@dowjones.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 4:16 (Dow Jones) US stocks climb more than 4% buoyed by coordinated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's cut to its bank-reserve requirements and a better-than-expected rise in ADP's private payrolls report. DJIA gains 490, or 4.2%, to 12045.68, its biggest rise since March 2009. S&P 500 ends up 52 at 1246.96 and the Nasdaq Comp climbs 104 to 2620.34. Still many analysts downplay today's moves as a true turning point and the move in Treasurys is not as pronounced with the 10-year yields up to 2.075%. (patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Call us at (212) 416-3100 or email patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2115 GMT [Dow Jones] A combination of leads, namely a sharply stronger AUD and weaker U.S. wheat futures, will weigh on Australian milling wheat Thursday. U.S. wheat futures ended lower overnight, failing to sustain strong early gains given the absence of supportive fundamental news. CBOT March wheat fell 2.0 cents to $6.14/bushel, while March KCBT wheat dropped 9.0 cents to $6.61 and March MGEX wheat lost 10.0 cents to $8.2350. The AUD/USD is at 1.0286, rocketing from 0.9950 late Wednesday. The most active ASX wheat futures deliverable January in New South Wales rose A$4.00 to A$224.50/metric ton Wednesday, while those in Western Australia were unchanged at A$228.00. ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 4:04 (Dow Jones) Treasury futures traders unloaded their relatively safe contracts Wednesday as high-risk stock market values soared when major central banks announced they will keep US dollars flowing in Europe's troubled banking system. They bounced from their lows later on, but the move had little to do with the Fed beige book's depiction of an economy that is slowly growing in all but one of its 12 regions. New lead-month March 10-yr Tsy notes tumbled early to a 1-month low of 128-28. They settled 12+/32 lower at 129-11, with contract's implied 10-yr rate above 2%. Mar classic Treasury bonds, covering 15-25yr maturities, settled 1 24/32 lower at 141-12, rebounding from this morning's 2-week low of 140-31. Mar ultra Tsy bonds, for maturities longer than 25yrs, settled 2 23/32 lower at 155-14.(howard.packowitz@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Call us at (212) 416-3100 or email bradley.davis@dowjones.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 November 30, 2011 17:41 ET (22:41 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020111130e7bu008dc\par }\page {\par\f