\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 841 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 November 2011\par 06:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par MOODY'S: LAUNCH OF IMF NEW CREDIT LINE POSITIVE FOR STRESSED EURO-ZONE SOVEREIGNS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (published 0354 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par REPORTS OF IMF PACKAGE FOR ITALY NOT CREDIBLE - INTERNATIONAL FINANCIAL OFFICIALS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A report that the International Monetary Fund could offer Italy between EUR400 billion and EUR600 billion in financial support is not credible, people familiar with ongoing international discussions on the European debt crisis said Monday. (published 0339 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par MOODY'S: PROBABILITY OF MULTIPLE DEFAULTS BY EURO-ZONE COUNTRIES NO LONGER NEGLIGIBLE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The probability of a series of defaults by euro-zone countries is no longer negligible, Moody's Investors Service warned Monday, noting that the longer the liquidity crisis continues, the more rapidly the risk of default would rise. (published 0451 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par SENIOR JAPAN MOF OFFICIAL:NOT RULING OUT POSSIBILITY OF SOLO \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 "There's no reason that Japan should be regarded as a safe-haven," given its own fiscal problems and slow pace of growth, Takehiko Nakao, Vice Minister of Finance for international affairs, said at the Paris Europlace International Financial Forum held in Tokyo. (published 0248 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BOJ SHIRAKAWA: JAPAN ECON EXPECTED TO FACE DIFFICULT SITUATION FOR WHILE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa said Monday the Japanese economy could face a difficult situation for a while, as the European debt crisis, the yen's continued strength and the flooding in Thailand could take a toll on exports. (published 0323 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA GOVERNMENT: EXPECT INFLATION TO STABILIZE FROM 2H 2012 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's Ministry of Strategy and Finance Monday said it expects inflation to stabilize from the second half of 2012. (published 0411 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA 3Q OVERSEAS INVESTMENTS DOWN 34.7% ON EURO-ZONE WOES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's third-quarter total overseas investments fell sharply from a year earlier, as companies cut spending amid heightened external uncertainties stemming from the euro-zone fiscal crisis. (published 0455 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ: EUROPE LEADERS ARE NEGOTIATING NEW FISCAL TIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro zone's leaders are negotiating a ground-breaking fiscal pact aimed at preventing the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bloc from fracturing by tethering its members even closer together. (published 0041 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par GERMANY WEIGHS IDEA OF CORE EURO-ZONE BONDS - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Germany is weighing the idea of joining with five other core euro-zone member states to issue common bonds in order to stabilize their high credit rating, German daily Die Welt reported Monday, citing sources close to the negotiations. (published 0422 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ECB NOYER: EFSF INCREASE UNREALISTIC \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governing council member Christian Noyer said Monday that an increase in the European Financial Stability Fund would be unrealistic, but he's open to any scheme that would allow existing commitment to be leveraged to provide greater \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  capacity. (published 0106 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CHINA\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VICE PREMIER: CHANCE OF LONG-TERM ECONOMIC WEAKNESS HAS RISEN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The chance of long-term global economic weakness has risen, Chinese Vice Premier Li Keqiang said in remarks published on a government website Monday, noting the spiralling European sovereign debt crisis, unstable international financial markets and slowing growth combined with price rises in some countries. (published 0122 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA COMMERCE MIN: UNCLEAR IF EU CAN SMOOTHLY IMPLEMENT BAILOUT PLANS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It is unclear if the European Union can smoothly implement the bailout plans it has outlined and how effective they would be, Chinese Commerce Minister Chen Deming said Monday. (published 0355 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA ECONOMIST: SEE CPI RISE AROUND 4% IN 2012 - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's consumer price index growth is likely to be around 5.5% in 2011 but will likely fall to around 4% in 2012, the Financial News reported Monday, citing Fan Jianping, an economist at the National Development and Reform Commission's state information center. (published 0057 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INTERVIEW: \f28 \uc2 \f2 DEUTSCHE BANK\f28 \uc2 \f2  EXPECTS DIM SUM BOND GROWTH TO SLOW 2012 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Growth in the market for offshore yuan-denominated bonds is likely to slow next year, as investors dial down their expectations about how much further the yuan will appreciate, a top banker from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  said. (published 0102 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par DATA SNAP: PHILIPPINES 3Q GDP +3.2% ON YEAR; MARKET EXPECTED +4.6% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Philippine economy expanded 3.2% in the third quarter from a year earlier, with unfavorable weather conditions, higher fuel prices and sluggish construction slowing domestic growth, the National Statistical Coordination Board said Monday. (published 0223 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par PREVIEW: KOREA NOVEMBER EXPORTS LIKELY REBOUNDED; CPI LIKELY ABOVE BOK TARGET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's exports growth likely rebounded in November but inflation for the month likely exceeded the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's target of 2% to 4% for the year, presenting difficult policy choices for Seoul as it faces heightened external uncertainties. (published 0449 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PREVIEW: AUSTRALIA'S SWAN TO WIELD SPENDING AXE TO PRESERVE SURPLUS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian Treasurer Wayne Swan is expected to wield the axe on spending this week when he announces a mid-year review of the government's 2011-12 budget, keeping alive a key election promise to produce a surplus by 2012-13. (published 0330 GMT) [ 28-11-11 0500GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111128e7bs0007l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
