\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 983 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 November 2011\par 12:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par HUNGARY TO START TALKS ON IMF DEAL FRIDAY, PM SAYS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BUDAPEST (Dow Jones)--Hungary will formally inform the International Monetary Fund Friday it wants to sign an insurance-type agreement, Prime Minister Viktor Orban said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par DATA SNAP: GERMAN OCTOBER PPI +0.2% ON MONTH, +5.3% ON YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FRANKFURT (Dow Jones)--Prices at the factory gate rose slightly more than expected in October in monthly terms, Germany's federal statistics office, Destatis, said Friday, driven by an increase in energy prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par GREECE SEES 2011 BUDGET DEFICIT AT 9%-BUDGET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ATHENS (Dow Jones)--Greece said Friday that it would miss its 2011 deficit targets with a gap equal to 9% of gross domestic product, and laid out two separate targets for next year depending on the outcome of a planned EUR100 billion debt write down.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par EBRD HEAD: EURO-ZONE DEBT CRISIS NOT LEADING TO GLOBAL RECESSION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FRANKFURT (Dow Jones)--The world isn't facing a recession as a result of the debt crisis in the euro zone, the head of the European Bank for Reconstruction and Development said Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ECB'S DRAGHI URGES EURO ZONE TO IMPLEMENT CRISIS DECISIONS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FRANKFURT (Dow Jones)--European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Mario Draghi Friday demanded swift implementation of measures agreed by euro-zone leaders in recent months to combat the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bloc's debt crisis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par DATA SNAP: ITALY INDUSTRIAL ORDERS SLUMP ON LOCAL DEMAND DROPOFF \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ROME (Dow Jones)--Italian industrial orders slumped in September, reversing the rise of the previous two months, and offering a further sign that the euro zone's third-largest economy is struggling, data showed Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ECB'S WEIDMANN: CRISIS DOESN'T JUSTIFY OVERSTEPPING ECB MANDATE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FRANKFURT (Dow Jones)--The euro zone's lack of success in resolving the debt and banking crisis doesn't justify overstepping or expanding the mandate of the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to help it fight the crisis, ECB Governing Council Member Jens Weidmann said Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA PREMIER: EMERGING MARKETS, DEVELOPING COUNTRIES FACE GROWING INFLATIONARY PRESSURE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BALI, Indonesia (Dow Jones)--China's Premier Wen Jiabao Friday said emerging markets and developing countries face growing inflationary pressures, while economic recovery in some developed countries lacks momentum due to their fiscal woe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA PROPERTY PRICES POST FIRST DECLINE SINCE GOVERNMENT CURBS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Average property prices in 70 Chinese cities covered in a government survey fell slightly in October from a month ago, recording their first on-month decline since Beijing started a two-year tightening campaign to cool the red-hot property market and suggesting the start of a much anticipated correction in prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA REAL HOUSEHOLD INCOME +1.6% ON YR IN 3Q VS +0.5% IN 2Q \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's real average household income rose at a faster pace in the third quarter compared with the second quarter on improved employment conditions, data from Statistics Korea showed Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \