\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 886 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 November 2011\par 11:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: GERMAN OCTOBER PPI +0.2% ON MONTH, +5.3% ON YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FRANKFURT (Dow Jones)--Prices at the factory gate rose slightly more than expected in October in monthly terms, Germany's federal statistics office, Destatis, said Friday, driven by an increase in energy prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par GREEK BUDGET DEFICIT MAY REACH 9.5% OF OUTPUT IN 2011 -REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ATHENS (Dow Jones)--Greece's budget deficit in 2011 is expected to widen up to 9.5% of economic output, compared with an initial target of 8.5%, a Greek newspaper reports Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par EBRD HEAD: EURO-ZONE DEBT CRISIS NOT LEADING TO GLOBAL RECESSION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FRANKFURT (Dow Jones)--The world isn't facing a recession as a result of the debt crisis in the euro zone, the head of the European Bank for Reconstruction and Development said Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ECB'S DRAGHI URGES EURO ZONE TO IMPLEMENT CRISIS DECISIONS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FRANKFURT (Dow Jones)--European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Mario Draghi Friday demanded swift implementation of measures agreed by euro-zone leaders in recent months to combat the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bloc's debt crisis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par DATA SNAP: ITALY INDUSTRIAL ORDERS SLUMP ON LOCAL DEMAND DROPOFF \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ROME (Dow Jones)--Italian industrial orders slumped in September, reversing the rise of the previous two months, and offering a further sign that the euro zone's third-largest economy is struggling, data showed Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA PREMIER: EMERGING MARKETS, DEVELOPING COUNTRIES FACE GROWING INFLATIONARY PRESSURE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BALI, Indonesia (Dow Jones)--China's Premier Wen Jiabao Friday said emerging markets and developing countries face growing inflationary pressures, while economic recovery in some developed countries lacks momentum due to their fiscal woe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA PROPERTY PRICES POST FIRST DECLINE SINCE GOVERNMENT CURBS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Average property prices in 70 Chinese cities covered in a government survey fell slightly in October from a month ago, recording their first on-month decline since Beijing started a two-year tightening campaign to cool the red-hot property market and suggesting the start of a much anticipated correction in prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA REAL HOUSEHOLD INCOME +1.6% ON YR IN 3Q VS +0.5% IN 2Q \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's real average household income rose at a faster pace in the third quarter compared with the second quarter on improved employment conditions, data from Statistics Korea showed Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par US LOCKE: CHINESE YUAN SHOULD FLOAT FREELY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should float freely, Gary Locke, U.S. ambassador to China, said Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PBOC ADVISER: CHINA SHOULD JOIN WITH IMF TO AID EUROPE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China should join forces with the International Monetary Fund and countries such as Russia to aid Europe, Li Daokui, an academic adviser to the People's Bank of China, said Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BOE WEALE: 'VERY STRONG CASE' FOR EXTENDING QE NEXT YEAR UNLESS OUTLOOK IMPROVES - FT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Martin Weale, an external member of the Bank of England's monetary policy committee, said there is a "very strong case" for extending the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's quantitative easing policy next year unless the outlook improves, the Financial Times reported on its website.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA PREMIER WEN: GLOBAL ECONOMIC UNCERTAINTIES, INSTABILITY MAY EXIST IN LONG TERM - XINHUA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's Premier Wen Jiabao said at a summit between China and the Association of Southeast Asian Nations Thursday that global economic uncertainties and instabilities may remain in the long term, adding that the two sides should move forward toward existing targets, the official Xinhua News Agency reported Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PBOC ADVISER: SHOULD USE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RESERVES TO \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  IN YUAN RATE - REPORT \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should use its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to adjust the yuan's exchange rate, People's Bank of China adviser Xia Bin was quoted as saying by the state-run Economic Information Daily Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par EX-PBOC ADVISER: CHINA 2012 GDP GROWTH LIKELY TO EASE TO 8.6%-8.7% - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's economic growth will likely ease to 8.6%-8.7% in 2012 and inflation will be controllable next year, the China Business News reported Friday, citing Fan Gang, a former adviser to China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par SINGAPORE MAS: RISKS TO FINANCIAL STABILITY INCREASED SIGNIFICANTLY IN 2H \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Risks to global financial stability have increased significantly in the second half of